// Rep Max questions: Business (Pearson Edexcel 1BS0), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gbu-001","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A baker starts selling vegan cakes because more customers now want plant-based food. What has caused this new business idea?","o":["A fall in interest rates","A new law on pricing","A merger with a rival","A change in customer tastes"],"a":3,"x":"New ideas often come from changing customer wants. Here, rising demand for plant-based food gave the baker an opportunity.","w":["Interest rates change borrowing costs, not what food customers want.","The question mentions no law; the cause is customers wanting plant-based food.","A merger joins firms together and doesn't explain a shift in what customers want.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-002","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Zara puts £10,000 of her own savings into a new shop. The shop fails after a year. Which risk does this show?","o":["Increased profit from lower costs","Business failure and loss of money invested","Gaining independence at work","Reward for taking a chance"],"a":1,"x":"Business failure is a key risk of enterprise. Entrepreneurs who invest their own money can lose it if the business fails.","w":["Lower costs and profit would be a reward, but Zara's shop failed and lost her money.","","Independence is a benefit of enterprise, not a risk.","Reward is the opposite of risk: Zara lost her savings."]}
{"id":"gbu-003","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A café buys ingredients costing £2 for each sandwich and sells it for £5. What is the value added per sandwich?","o":["£3","£2","£5","£7"],"a":0,"x":"Added value is selling price minus the cost of bought-in inputs: £5 - £2 = £3.","w":["","£2 is the input cost, not the value added.","£5 is the selling price; you must subtract the £2 of ingredients.","Added cost to price instead of subtracting."]}
{"id":"gbu-004","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A customer chooses a shop because it is close to home and open late. Which customer need is this?","o":["Quality","Choice","Convenience","Price"],"a":2,"x":"Convenience means making buying easy, for example by being nearby or open at useful times.","w":["Quality is about how good the product is, not location or opening hours.","Choice is about the range on offer, not being close and open late.","","Price is about cost; the question talks about location and hours."]}
{"id":"gbu-005","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of primary market research?","o":["Government population statistics","A questionnaire given to local customers","A report bought from a research firm","Articles found on the internet"],"a":1,"x":"Primary research is new data collected first-hand, such as surveys or interviews. The other options use existing secondary data.","w":["Government statistics are secondary data, collected by someone else.","","A bought report is secondary research, not collected first-hand.","Internet articles are secondary; others gathered that data."]}
{"id":"gbu-006","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of segmenting a market by age?","o":["Selling gym membership to people on high incomes","Selling coats to people who live in cold areas","Selling toys for 3 to 5 year olds","Selling football kits to fans"],"a":2,"x":"Segmentation by age splits customers into age groups. The other options use income, location or lifestyle.","w":["That segments by income, not age.","That segments by location, not age.","","Football fans is a lifestyle or interest segment, not an age group."]}
{"id":"gbu-007","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A market map of trainers shows no brands at high price and high quality. What might this gap suggest to a new business?","o":["It proves no customers would ever buy there","The market is already full","It no longer needs any market research","There may be an opportunity to sell a product there"],"a":3,"x":"A gap on a market map may show an opportunity. It does not prove there is demand, so research is still needed to check enough customers would buy.","w":["A gap doesn't prove nobody would buy; it may show an opportunity.","A gap means that part of the market is empty, not full.","Research is still needed to check that enough customers would buy.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-008","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A new florist opens next to a supermarket that sells cheap flowers. Which choice is most likely to help the florist compete?","o":["Offer better quality and expert, personal service","Sell the same basic bunches as the supermarket","Stock a smaller range of flowers","Open for fewer hours each week"],"a":0,"x":"A small florist is unlikely to beat a supermarket on price. It competes where the supermarket is weaker: quality, product range and customer service.","w":["","Selling the same basic bunches means a small shop can't win on price.","A smaller range removes a way to stand out from the supermarket.","Fewer opening hours makes the florist less convenient for customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-009","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is a non-financial objective of a new business?","o":["Making a profit of £20,000","Gaining independence","Raising sales by 10%","Winning 5% of the market"],"a":1,"x":"Independence is a non-financial aim. Profit, sales and market share targets are financial objectives.","w":["A £20,000 profit is a financial objective.","","Sales growth is a financial objective.","Market share is a financial objective too."]}
{"id":"gbu-010","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"A stall sells 240 scarves at £15 each. What is the revenue?","o":["£3,600","£255","£360","£2,400"],"a":0,"x":"Revenue = price x quantity sold = £15 x 240 = £3,600.","w":["","Added the price and quantity instead of multiplying.","Place-value slip: that is $15 \\times 24$, not $15 \\times 240$.","Multiplied 240 by 10 instead of by 15."]}
{"id":"gbu-011","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Fixed costs are £6,000. Each unit sells for £5 and has variable costs of £2. What is the break-even output?","o":["1,200 units","3,000 units","857 units","2,000 units"],"a":3,"x":"Break-even = fixed costs / (price - variable cost per unit) = 6,000 / 3 = 2,000. Using the price alone gives 1,200.","w":["Divided by the price alone and forgot to subtract variable cost.","Divided by the variable cost of £2 instead of the £3 contribution.","Added price and variable cost ($5 + 2$) instead of subtracting.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-012","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A business breaks even at 400 units a month. It sells 650 units. What is its margin of safety?","o":["400 units","1,050 units","250 units","150 units"],"a":2,"x":"Margin of safety = actual sales - break-even output = 650 - 400 = 250 units.","w":["That is the break-even output, not the margin of safety.","Added sales and break-even instead of subtracting.","","Subtraction slip: $650 - 400 = 250$, not 150."]}
{"id":"gbu-013","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Fixed costs are £9,000. Price is £12 and variable cost is £7 per unit. The business sells 2,400 units. What is its profit?","o":["£12,000","£28,800","£2,400","£3,000"],"a":3,"x":"Profit per unit before fixed costs is £5. 2,400 x £5 = £12,000, then take off £9,000 = £3,000. £12,000 forgets fixed costs.","w":["Forgot to take the £9,000 fixed costs off the £12,000.","Found revenue (2,400 × £12) and ignored all the costs.","Just reused the number of units sold; it isn't a profit calculation.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-014","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Why can a profitable business still run out of cash?","o":["Profit and cash are always the same","Profit is paid out as tax on the day it is earned","Customers may pay late while bills must be paid now","Cash only matters to large businesses"],"a":2,"x":"Profit is not the same as cash. Timing matters: if money comes in later than payments go out, a business can run short of cash.","w":["Profit and cash are different; timing can leave a firm short of cash.","Tax isn't paid on the day profit is earned, and that isn't the issue here.","","Cash flow matters to every business, large or small."]}
{"id":"gbu-015","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":3,"q":"January: opening £1,000, inflows £4,000, outflows £3,200. February: inflows £3,500, outflows £4,300. What is February's closing balance?","o":["£1,000","-£800","£1,800","£800"],"a":0,"x":"January closing = 1,000 + 4,000 - 3,200 = £1,800. That is February's opening. Then 1,800 + 3,500 - 4,300 = £1,000.","w":["","Used only February's net flow (3,500 − 4,300) and forgot the £1,800 opening balance.","That's February's opening balance; you stopped before adding February's flows.","Took inflows from outflows the wrong way round and ignored the opening balance."]}
{"id":"gbu-016","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A small shop must pay a supplier now, but its own customers will not pay it for two weeks. Which source of finance best covers this short gap?","o":["A five-year bank loan","A bank overdraft","Share capital from new owners","Venture capital"],"a":1,"x":"An overdraft is a short-term source of finance for brief cash gaps. Loans, share capital and venture capital are long-term sources, used for bigger needs over longer periods.","w":["A five-year loan is too long and costly for a two-week gap.","","Share capital is long-term and gives away ownership; not for a short gap.","Venture capital funds long-term growth, not a two-week gap."]}
{"id":"gbu-017","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Which business type has unlimited liability and one owner?","o":["Private limited company","Public limited company","Sole trader","Partnership"],"a":2,"x":"A sole trader has one owner who is personally liable for the business's debts. A partnership also has unlimited liability but two or more owners. Companies have limited liability.","w":["Private limited companies have limited liability, not unlimited.","Plcs have limited liability and many shareholders.","","Partnerships have unlimited liability but two or more owners, not one."]}
{"id":"gbu-018","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A bakery wants to move to a place where it will get lots of passing customers. Which location factor is this?","o":["Proximity to the market","Proximity to raw materials","Availability of labour","Costs of premises"],"a":0,"x":"Being near customers matters for shops that rely on footfall. Other factors include materials, workers and costs.","w":["","Raw materials matter to manufacturers, not a shop wanting passing trade.","Labour supply is about finding staff, not attracting customers.","Premises costs are about rent and expense, not footfall."]}
{"id":"gbu-019","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is part of the marketing mix?","o":["Profit","Promotion","Production","People management"],"a":1,"x":"The four Ps are product, price, promotion and place.","w":["Profit is a business outcome, not one of the four Ps.","","Production is an operations activity, not one of the four Ps.","People management is HR, not part of the marketing mix."]}
{"id":"gbu-020","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":2,"q":"Why does a bank often ask for a business plan before lending money?","o":["It proves the business will definitely make a profit","It removes all risk that the loan will not be repaid","It replaces the need for the owner to do market research","It shows plans and forecasts so the bank can judge the risk"],"a":3,"x":"A business plan sets out aims, market research, forecasts and finance needs. Forecasts are not guarantees, but they help a lender judge risk, and planning helps the owner reduce risk.","w":["Forecasts are only estimates; they can't prove a profit.","Risk can't be removed completely; a plan only helps judge it.","The plan is built on market research; it doesn't replace it.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-021","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"Shareholders want higher dividends but employees want higher wages. What does this show?","o":["A shortage of cash","A rise in revenue","A conflict between stakeholders","A change in the law"],"a":2,"x":"Stakeholders have different aims. Paying more to one group can reduce what is available for another.","w":["Nothing says cash is short; the issue is two groups wanting different things.","Nothing here says revenue is rising; the issue is competing demands.","","No law is changing; the groups simply have different aims."]}
{"id":"gbu-022","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"What is e-commerce?","o":["Selling only in high-street shops","Printing leaflets for customers","Making goods using machines","Buying and selling goods and services online"],"a":3,"x":"E-commerce means trading over the internet, for example through a website or app.","w":["That's traditional high-street retail, not online trading.","Leaflets are print marketing, not trading online.","That's production, not buying and selling online.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-023","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A shop sells a toaster that stops working after two days. Under consumer law, what must goods be?","o":["Of satisfactory quality and fit for purpose","Cheaper than rival goods","Made in the UK","Sold only by large firms"],"a":0,"x":"The Consumer Rights Act says goods must be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose and as described. Faulty goods can be returned.","w":["","Consumer law doesn't require goods to be cheaper than rivals.","No law says goods must be made in the UK.","Consumer law covers every seller, large or small."]}
{"id":"gbu-024","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":3,"q":"The pound becomes stronger against the euro. A UK firm exports to Europe and keeps its prices in pounds the same. What is the likely effect?","o":["Its goods become cheaper for European buyers","Its goods become more expensive for European buyers","Its UK costs fall by the same amount","Its profits always rise"],"a":1,"x":"A stronger pound means euro buyers need more euros to pay the same £ price, so exports can become less competitive.","w":["Reversed: a stronger pound makes exports dearer for euro buyers, not cheaper.","","UK costs are in pounds, so they don't fall because of the exchange rate.","Higher prices abroad may cut sales, so profits won't always rise."]}
{"id":"gbu-025","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":2,"q":"Which is an example of a business responding to a change in technology?","o":["A café raises prices after the legal minimum wage goes up","A bank closes branches as more customers bank on their phones","A builder delays borrowing after interest rates rise","A shop cuts prices as unemployment rises in its area"],"a":1,"x":"External changes include technology, legislation and the economic climate. Customers moving to phone banking is a technology change. The minimum wage is legislation; interest rates and unemployment are the economic climate.","w":["Minimum wage is a law change (legislation), not a technology change.","","Interest rates are part of the economic climate, not technology.","Unemployment is the economic climate, not technology."]}
{"id":"gbu-026","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A private limited company becomes a plc. What can it now do?","o":["Avoid paying corporation tax on profit","Give its owners unlimited liability","Stop publishing its accounts each year","Sell shares to the public on the stock exchange"],"a":3,"x":"A plc can raise large sums by selling shares to the public. Its shareholders keep limited liability, and it must publish more information about itself.","w":["Plcs still pay corporation tax on profit; becoming a plc changes nothing here.","Wrong way round: shareholders in a plc keep limited liability.","Plcs must publish more information, not less, so this is the opposite.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-027","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A drinks firm sells mostly to teenagers. A new law bans selling its main drink to under-16s, so it changes its aim from growth to survival straight away. What caused this change?","o":["Technology","Internal reasons, such as a new owner","Legislation","Business performance"],"a":2,"x":"Aims can change because of market conditions, technology, performance, legislation or internal reasons. Here a new law forced the change, so the cause is legislation.","w":["No new technology is mentioned; the cause is the new law.","No internal change like a new owner is given; the law came from outside.","","Nothing suggests poor results; the ban was imposed from outside."]}
{"id":"gbu-028","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What is an import?","o":["A good or service bought from another country","A good or service sold to another country","A business with branches in many countries","A tax on goods coming into a country"],"a":0,"x":"Imports are bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. A multinational operates in several countries, and a tax on imports is a tariff.","w":["","Mixed up with an export, which is sold abroad.","This describes a multinational, not an import.","This is a tariff, the tax on imports, not the import itself."]}
{"id":"gbu-029","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A firm pays suppliers fairly and avoids damaging the environment. Which is a likely benefit?","o":["Lower costs for the business in every case","Guaranteed higher profits every year","An end to competition from rivals","A better reputation that attracts customers"],"a":3,"x":"Ethical behaviour can improve reputation and customer loyalty. It may also raise costs, so higher profit is not guaranteed: there can be a trade-off between ethics and profit.","w":["Ethical choices often cost more, so costs are not always lower.","Profit is never guaranteed; ethics can raise costs and cut profit.","Rivals still compete; ethical behaviour does not remove competition.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-030","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm relaunches a falling product with a new flavour and packaging. What is this called?","o":["An extension strategy","Market research","A merger","Break-even"],"a":0,"x":"Extension strategies such as new packaging, new uses or price changes aim to lengthen the product's life cycle.","w":["","Market research finds out about customers; it is not relaunching a product.","A merger joins two firms, not a relaunch of one product.","Break-even is where revenue equals costs, not a relaunch."]}
{"id":"gbu-031","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A product costs £8 to make. The business adds a 40% mark-up using cost-plus pricing. What is the selling price?","o":["£8.40","£12.80","£11.20","£13.33"],"a":2,"x":"Mark-up = 40% of £8 = £3.20. Selling price = £8 + £3.20 = £11.20.","w":["Added 40p, not 40% of £8, which is £3.20.","Added 60% rather than 40%; mark-up is 40% of £8 = £3.20.","","Divided £8 by 0.6, treating 40% as a profit margin, not a mark-up on cost."]}
{"id":"gbu-032","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a digital method of promotion?","o":["Printing a newspaper advert","Posting adverts on social media","Handing out leaflets","Putting up a poster"],"a":1,"x":"Digital promotion uses online channels such as social media, email and websites.","w":["A printed newspaper advert is traditional print media, not digital.","","Leaflets are printed, not online, so not digital.","Posters are printed, not online, so not digital."]}
{"id":"gbu-033","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"What is an e-tailer?","o":["A shop with a high-street store","A retailer that sells goods online","A firm that makes goods","A firm that stores goods"],"a":1,"x":"E-tailers sell directly to customers over the internet. Retailers use shops.","w":["That is a high-street retailer; an e-tailer sells online.","","That describes a manufacturer, not a seller to the public.","That describes a warehouse or storage firm, not a seller."]}
{"id":"gbu-034","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A firm launches a premium watch. Each option gives product, price, promotion and place. Which marketing mix fits together best?","o":["High quality, low price, luxury magazine adverts, exclusive shops","High quality, high price, luxury magazine adverts, discount stores","High quality, high price, luxury magazine adverts, exclusive shops","High quality, high price, money-off coupons, exclusive shops"],"a":2,"x":"Each element of the mix should support the others. A premium watch needs a high price, upmarket promotion and exclusive outlets. A low price, coupons or discount stores would clash with its premium image.","w":["A low price clashes with a premium, high-quality image.","Discount stores clash with a premium image; place should be exclusive.","","Money-off coupons cheapen the image; promotion should be luxury."]}
{"id":"gbu-035","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which type of production makes one unique item to a customer's order?","o":["Job production","Batch production","Flow production","Mass production"],"a":0,"x":"Job production makes one-off products, such as a bespoke wedding dress. Batch makes groups of identical items, flow makes continuously.","w":["","Batch makes groups of identical items, not one unique item.","Flow runs continuously for identical items, not one-offs.","Mass production makes lots of identical items, not a unique one."]}
{"id":"gbu-036","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A business uses just-in-time stock control. What is a benefit?","o":["No risk of late deliveries","Higher stock-holding costs","Bigger bulk-buying discounts","Lower storage costs"],"a":3,"x":"JIT means getting supplies only when needed, which cuts storage costs. It relies on reliable suppliers, and smaller, frequent orders usually mean losing bulk-buying discounts.","w":["JIT depends on suppliers, so late deliveries are a real risk.","JIT holds little stock, so stock-holding costs fall, not rise.","Small frequent orders usually lose bulk-buying discounts.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-037","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A bakery makes every worker check quality at each stage, to stop faults happening, rather than only inspecting the finished loaves. What is this approach?","o":["Quality control","Quality assurance","Just-in-time stock control","Flow production"],"a":1,"x":"Quality assurance checks quality at every stage to prevent faults. Quality control inspects finished products to find faults. Both help a business control costs and gain a competitive advantage.","w":["Mixes up the two: control inspects finished goods, this checks every stage.","","Just-in-time is about stock arriving when needed, not checking quality.","Flow production is a continuous production line, not a quality approach."]}
{"id":"gbu-038","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of after-sales service?","o":["Offering free samples before purchase","Putting up a window display","Running an advert","Repairing a product after it is bought"],"a":3,"x":"After-sales service happens once the customer has bought, for example guarantees, repairs and returns help.","w":["Free samples come before purchase, so they are promotion, not after-sales.","Window displays attract customers before they buy, so not after-sales.","Adverts aim to win sales beforehand, so they are not after-sales service.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-039","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A business has revenue of £80,000 and cost of sales of £52,000. What is its gross profit margin?","o":["65%","54%","35%","28%"],"a":2,"x":"Gross profit = 80,000 - 52,000 = £28,000. Gross profit margin = 28,000 / 80,000 x 100 = 35%.","w":["Worked out cost of sales as a % of revenue (52 ÷ 80), not gross profit.","Divided gross profit by cost of sales (28 ÷ 52) instead of by revenue.","","Found the gross profit (£28,000) but never turned it into a % of revenue."]}
{"id":"gbu-040","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"An investment of £40,000 earns total profit of £30,000 over 4 years. What is the average rate of return (ARR)?","o":["18.75%","75%","187.5%","7.5%"],"a":0,"x":"Average annual profit = 30,000 / 4 = £7,500. ARR = 7,500 / 40,000 x 100 = 18.75%.","w":["","Forgot to divide the total profit by 4 years to get the average yearly profit.","Place-value slip: 7,500 ÷ 40,000 × 100 gives 18.75%, not 187.5%.","Took the average profit (£7,500) as 7.5% without dividing by £40,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-041","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":3,"q":"Project A costs £50,000 and earns £60,000 total profit over 5 years. Project B costs £30,000 and earns £42,000 total profit over 6 years. Which has the higher ARR?","o":["B, at 23.3%","A, at 120%","B, at 140%","A, at 24%"],"a":3,"x":"A: 60,000 / 5 = 12,000, which is 24% of 50,000. B: 42,000 / 6 = 7,000, which is 23.3% of 30,000. A is higher.","w":["Worked B out correctly, but 23.3% is lower than A's 24%.","Forgot to divide by 5 years: 60,000 ÷ 50,000 is the total return, not ARR.","Forgot to divide by 6 years, so B's ARR looks far too big.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-042","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"Revenue rose from £100,000 to £120,000, but net profit fell from £10,000 to £9,000. What happened to the net profit margin?","o":["It fell from 10% to 7.5%","It rose from 10% to 12%","It stayed at 10%","It fell from 10% to 9%"],"a":0,"x":"Margin was 10,000 / 100,000 = 10%. Now it is 9,000 / 120,000 = 7.5%. Higher revenue does not mean higher margin.","w":["","Assumed higher revenue means a higher margin; profit actually fell.","Assumed the margin stays the same; 9,000 ÷ 120,000 is now only 7.5%.","Divided £9,000 by the old revenue (£100,000), not the new £120,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-043","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Compared with a hierarchical (tall) structure, a flat organisational structure has...","o":["More layers of management and a narrow span of control","Fewer layers of management and a wide span of control","Fewer layers of management and a narrow span of control","More layers of management and a wide span of control"],"a":1,"x":"A flat structure has few levels between top and bottom, so each manager oversees more staff (a wide span of control) and communication is quicker.","w":["That describes a tall structure: many layers and a narrow span of control.","","Mixed up span: fewer layers means each manager controls more staff, so wide.","Mixed up: more layers gives a narrow span, not a wide one."]}
{"id":"gbu-044","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":1,"q":"Which recruitment document lists the tasks and responsibilities of a job?","o":["Curriculum vitae","Application form","Job description","Person specification"],"a":2,"x":"A job description sets out the tasks and duties of the role. A person specification describes the skills and qualities wanted; the CV and application form come from the applicant.","w":["A CV is written by the applicant to show their own history, not job tasks.","An application form is filled in by the applicant; it doesn't list job tasks.","","A person specification lists the skills and qualities wanted, not the tasks."]}
{"id":"gbu-045","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A new worker is shown how to use the tills by a colleague while serving customers. What is this?","o":["Formal training","A performance review","Self-learning","Informal training"],"a":3,"x":"Informal training happens while working, learning from an experienced colleague. Formal training is planned and structured, such as a course. Self-learning is when staff teach themselves.","w":["Formal training is planned and structured, like a course, not learning on the job.","A performance review assesses how someone is doing; it doesn't teach the tills.","Self-learning means teaching yourself; here a colleague shows the worker.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-046","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is a non-financial way to motivate staff?","o":["A bonus","Job rotation","Piece rate","Commission"],"a":1,"x":"Job rotation moves staff between tasks for variety. Bonuses, piece rate and commission are financial.","w":["A bonus is extra money, so it is a financial motivator.","","Piece rate pays per item made, so it is financial, not non-financial.","Commission is pay based on sales, so it is financial."]}
{"id":"gbu-047","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A worker is paid £6 per item (piece rate) plus a £20 bonus for quality. She makes 35 items. What does she earn?","o":["£210","£255","£230","£720"],"a":2,"x":"Piece rate pay = 35 x £6 = £210. Add the £20 bonus to get £230. Piece rate is a financial motivator linked to output.","w":["Worked out the piece rate (35 × £6) but left out the £20 bonus.","","",""]}
{"id":"gbu-048","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm's gross profit margin rose from 30% to 35%, but its net profit margin fell from 8% to 6%. What is the most likely reason?","o":["Overheads and expenses rose faster than gross profit","Cost of sales rose faster than revenue","Interest and other expenses fell","Selling prices were cut sharply"],"a":0,"x":"Gross margin rose, so cost of sales was controlled. Net margin falling means overheads such as rent and wages rose by more than the gain.","w":["","If cost of sales rose faster than revenue, gross margin would fall, but it rose.","Lower interest and expenses would raise net margin, not lower it.","Price cuts would squeeze gross margin, yet it rose from 30% to 35%."]}
{"id":"gbu-049","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A shop rents out DVDs. Most customers now use streaming, so few want DVDs. What word describes a product no longer wanted because newer technology has replaced it?","o":["Adapted","Obsolete","Seasonal","Original"],"a":1,"x":"A product that newer technology has replaced is obsolete. The trap is confusing it with 'adapted', which means an existing product has been changed, not that it is out of date.","w":["An adapted product is an existing one that has been changed; it does not mean the product is out of date.","","Seasonal products sell only at certain times of year; the DVDs are not wanted because of technology.","An original idea is brand new, which is the opposite of an out-of-date product."]}
{"id":"gbu-050","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which one is an example of an ADAPTED business idea?","o":["An inventor launches a product nobody has sold before","A shop keeps selling the same goods in the same way","A firm shuts a loss-making branch to cut its costs","A bike maker adds an electric motor to its existing bikes"],"a":3,"x":"An adapted idea changes an existing product or service, such as adding a motor to a bike. An original idea is brand new. Cutting costs or changing nothing is not a new idea.","w":["This is an original idea because the product is brand new, not a change to an existing one.","Changing nothing creates no new or adapted idea.","Closing a branch to save money is a cost decision, not a new or adapted idea.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-051","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm prints road maps. Free sat-nav apps on phones now do the same job. Which statement is correct?","o":["Technology is making its main product obsolete, so it needs new ideas","Demand for its maps will rise because the apps are free","The need to find routes has gone, so no new product could succeed","This is a one-off change, so the market will stay the same"],"a":0,"x":"New technology can make a product obsolete. Markets are dynamic, so firms must adapt or find new ideas. The trap is thinking the customer need has gone; people still need routes, just delivered differently.","w":["","Free apps give customers a cheaper alternative, so demand for printed maps is more likely to fall.","People still need to find routes, so the need remains and new or adapted products could meet it.","Markets keep changing as technology and wants change, so assuming it will stay the same is wrong."]}
{"id":"gbu-052","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A small firm uses 3D printers to make phone cases with each customer's own design. How did new technology help to create this business idea?","o":["It made customers start wanting phone cases","It removed all competition from the market","It made one-off custom designs cheap to produce","It guaranteed the firm would make a profit"],"a":2,"x":"3D printing made one-off custom items cheap enough to sell, so technology opened up a new opportunity. The trap is assuming technology creates demand or guarantees success; it only makes the idea possible.","w":["Technology does not force demand; customers already buy phone cases and want something personal.","Other firms can use the same technology, so competition is not removed.","","No idea guarantees profit; technology only makes the idea possible, and costs and sales still matter."]}
{"id":"gbu-053","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Which new business idea came about because of BOTH a change in technology AND a change in what customers want?","o":["A café opens a second branch after a nearby rival shuts down","A firm launches a home-workout app as smartphones and home exercise grow","A shop cuts its prices after its supplier lowers its costs","A bakery copies a rival's cake recipe and sells it the same way"],"a":1,"x":"The app depends on smartphone technology and on customers wanting to exercise at home. The other options come from a rival closing, lower costs, or copying, not both causes.","w":["This comes from a rival leaving the market, not a change in technology or customer wants.","","This is a pricing decision after lower costs; it is not a new idea from technology or customer wants.","Copying a recipe in the same way is not a new idea and has no technology or customer-want change."]}
{"id":"gbu-054","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a reward of running your own business?","o":["A guaranteed wage every month","Protection from ever losing money","Keeping the profit left after costs are paid","Working hours set by a manager"],"a":2,"x":"Profit is a key reward of enterprise. Owners keep what is left after costs. A guaranteed wage and set hours are features of being an employee, and owners can lose money.","w":["A guaranteed wage belongs to employees. Owners only earn if the business makes enough money, so this is a mix-up.","Owners can lose money, and failure is a risk. Thinking the business is protected from loss is a misconception.","","Having a manager set your hours is being an employee. Choosing your own hours is the independence reward of enterprise."]}
{"id":"gbu-055","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Dev leaves his job to open a bakery. For three months sales are too low and he cannot pay himself anything. Which risk does this show?","o":["Lack of security","Business failure","Loss of independence","Increased profit"],"a":0,"x":"Lack of security means the owner has no regular, guaranteed income and may earn little or nothing at first. The bakery is still open, so it has not failed. Do not confuse low income with failure.","w":["","The bakery is still trading, so it has not failed. Mixing up low early income with closure is the slip.","Dev still makes his own decisions. Independence is a reward, not something lost by low sales.","Profit is a reward. Dev is earning nothing, so profit has not increased."]}
{"id":"gbu-056","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A sole trader sells 800 items at £75 each. Her total costs are £46,200. What is the result, and what does it show?","o":["£13,800 loss, a risk of running a business","£13,800 profit, a reward of running a business","£106,200 profit, a reward of running a business","£46,200 profit, a reward of running a business"],"a":1,"x":"Revenue is 800 × £75 = £60,000. Profit = £60,000 − £46,200 = £13,800. Revenue is higher than costs, so this is a profit, which is a reward. Adding costs to revenue is a trap.","w":["The amount is right, but revenue is higher than costs, so it is a profit, not a loss. Reversing the subtraction is the slip.","","This adds costs to revenue (£60,000 + £46,200). Profit is found by subtracting costs from revenue.","This is just the costs figure. Profit is revenue minus costs, not the costs on their own."]}
{"id":"gbu-057","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Sam can open a shop selling a well-known snack, or launch a new untested gadget that could make much more profit. Which statement is correct?","o":["The gadget is lower risk because a new product has no competitors","Both ideas have the same risk because both are shops","The snack has the higher reward because its sales are certain","The gadget has higher risk but a higher possible reward"],"a":3,"x":"Untested products may not sell, so the risk of failure is higher. If they do sell well, the profit could be much greater. Higher risk is often linked with a higher possible reward.","w":["No competitors does not mean no risk. Customers may not want an untested product, so the risk is higher.","The risk differs. An unproven product is riskier than a proven one, whatever type of business sells it.","Sales of a known snack are more likely, but not certain. The reward is usually lower than a successful new idea.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-059","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a way a business can add value to a product?","o":["Paying a higher rent for its premises","Building a strong brand so customers will pay more","Buying a larger amount of raw materials","Employing extra staff in the office"],"a":1,"x":"Value is added when customers will pay more than the cost of inputs. A strong brand does this. Higher costs alone, such as rent or wages, do not add value; that is a common trap.","w":["Higher rent is a cost. It does not make customers pay more, so confusing cost with value is the slip.","","Buying more materials raises costs. It adds value only if customers pay more for the finished product.","Extra staff raise costs. This does not by itself make the product worth more to customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-060","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"An entrepreneur is said to 'organise resources'. Which statement describes this role?","o":["Setting the rate of tax that every business must pay","Doing every single job in the business on their own","Buying shares in large companies to earn dividends","Combining money, people, equipment and materials to make a product"],"a":3,"x":"Entrepreneurs organise the factors of production: they combine finance, staff, equipment and materials to produce goods or services. They do not need to do every job themselves.","w":["Tax rates are set by the government, not by entrepreneurs. This mixes up the role of business and government.","Entrepreneurs usually hire others and organise them. Thinking they must do everything alone is a misconception.","Buying shares is investing, not organising resources to run a business.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-061","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A shop at a railway station sells bottled water for £1.20. A supermarket sells the same water for 60p. Why can the station shop charge more?","o":["Customers value the convenience of buying it there and then","The water is always of a higher quality","The station shop makes the water itself","Prices are always higher in smaller shops"],"a":0,"x":"Convenience adds value. Travellers pay extra to buy quickly at the point of need. The trap is assuming a higher price means better quality when the product is identical.","w":["","The water is the same, so quality is not the reason. Assuming a high price means high quality is the slip.","Nothing suggests the shop makes the water. It buys it in and adds value through location.","This is a false general rule. The reason here is the convenience customers value."]}
{"id":"gbu-062","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Aisha invests £5,000 of her savings in a new bakery. Which statement shows the RISK she takes as an entrepreneur?","o":["She hires a part-time helper for weekends","She keeps all the profit if the bakery does well","She could lose her savings if sales are too low","She chooses which flour to buy"],"a":2,"x":"Risk is the chance of losing something, such as money invested, if the business fails. Keeping profit is the reward. Hiring and choosing suppliers are decisions or organising resources.","w":["Hiring a helper is organising resources, not a risk.","Keeping profit is the reward, not the risk. Mixing up risk and reward is the slip.","","Choosing a supplier is a business decision, not the risk of losing money."]}
{"id":"gbu-063","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A firm buys plain T-shirts at £6 and prints designs on them, selling each at £15. It then adds a brand label costing £1 and raises the price to £20. How much extra value does the label add per T-shirt?","o":["£5","£4","£13","£9"],"a":1,"x":"Added value is price minus input costs. Before: £15 - £6 = £9. After: £20 - £7 = £13. The label adds £13 - £9 = £4. The trap is ignoring the extra £1 cost.","w":["£5 is only the price rise. It ignores the extra £1 cost of the label.","","£13 is the total added value after the label, not the extra value the label adds.","£9 is the added value before the label was added."]}
{"id":"gbu-064","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A café adds vegan and gluten-free dishes to its menu. Which customer need is it mainly trying to meet?","o":["Convenience","Choice","Price","Quality"],"a":1,"x":"A wider range of products gives customers more choice. The trap is mixing up choice (the range on offer) with quality (how good each item is).","w":["Convenience is about easy buying, such as location or hours; new dishes do not change this.","","Price is about cost; the café has not changed what it charges.","Quality is how good the food is; adding new dishes widens the range instead."]}
{"id":"gbu-065","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Why is it important for a business to understand its customers' needs?","o":["It guarantees the business will always make a profit","It lets the business charge the highest price possible","It means the business no longer has to compete with rivals","It helps the business sell what customers want, so sales rise"],"a":3,"x":"Businesses that match what customers want are more likely to sell and survive. The trap is overclaiming: it does not guarantee profit, because costs and rivals still matter.","w":["This overclaims, because costs and competitors still matter, so profit is never guaranteed.","Price is one of the needs, so very high prices push customers to rivals.","Rivals still compete for the same customers, whatever the business knows about them.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-066","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Café customers say they want fresh, well-made food and will pay a bit more for it. The owner buys cheaper ingredients to cut prices. Which customer need is being ignored?","o":["Quality","Price","Choice","Convenience"],"a":0,"x":"Customers asked for quality and accepted a higher price. Cheaper ingredients lower quality. The trap is assuming lower prices always please customers.","w":["","Price was actually lowered, so this need was not ignored; these customers ranked it lower.","Nothing in the story changes the range of food on offer.","Nothing in the story is about location, speed or opening hours."]}
{"id":"gbu-067","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A budget supermarket stocks far fewer products than its rivals but has very low prices. Why can it still win sales?","o":["Fewer products always means higher quality","Low prices mean it meets customers' need for convenience","Many customers value low prices more than a wide choice","Customers never care about the range of products"],"a":2,"x":"Customer needs differ in importance. Shoppers who put price first may accept less choice. The trap is thinking every customer wants every need met equally.","w":["Range size does not decide quality; this confuses choice with quality.","Low prices meet the price need, not convenience, which is about easy buying.","","'Never' is too strong; some customers do value choice, but these ones value price more."]}
{"id":"gbu-068","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A survey of a bakery's customers finds they want cheap bread and somewhere to buy it on the way to work. Which decision best meets BOTH needs?","o":["Sell premium sourdough at higher prices from a quiet back street","Open a stall at the station selling bread cheaper than rivals","Add 50 new types of cake to the range at the current price","Sell bread online at the same price with a delivery fee"],"a":1,"x":"A station stall is on the way to work (convenience) and low prices meet the price need. The trap is picking an option that meets only one need, or a need customers did not mention.","w":["This focuses on quality, but raises the price and is off the commuters' route, so it misses both needs.","","This widens choice, which customers did not ask for, and does nothing about location or lower prices.","A delivery fee raises the cost, so the price need is not met, and it is not on the way to work."]}
{"id":"gbu-069","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A new business has a small budget and needs market information quickly. Why might secondary research suit it?","o":["It is collected first-hand to answer the firm's own questions","It is always more accurate than primary research","It already exists, so it is usually quicker and cheaper to get","It lets the firm choose exactly who to ask"],"a":2,"x":"Secondary data has already been collected and published, so it is usually cheaper and faster. The trap is thinking it is first-hand or tailored. It may be out of date or not specific.","w":["That describes primary research. Secondary data was collected by someone else, not first-hand.","Secondary data is not always more accurate. It can be out of date or collected for another purpose.","","The firm cannot choose who is asked in secondary research. That is a feature of primary research."]}
{"id":"gbu-070","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of qualitative data from market research?","o":["A customer says, \"The bottle looks fun but feels flimsy.\"","72% of those asked prefer the blue bottle","The average customer spends £3.80","250 people took part in the survey"],"a":0,"x":"Qualitative data is descriptive: opinions, feelings and reasons in words. Percentages, averages and counts are numbers, so they are quantitative. The trap is thinking any survey result is qualitative.","w":["","A percentage is a number, so it is quantitative data, not opinions in words.","An average is a number, so it is quantitative data.","A count of people is a number, so it is quantitative data."]}
{"id":"gbu-071","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A café owner asks 10 friends in his café if he should open a second café. Which change would make his research most reliable?","o":["Ask the same 10 friends again next week","Ask only the existing customers of his café","Use only closed questions with the same 10 friends","Ask a larger random sample from his target market"],"a":3,"x":"A larger, random sample from the target market is more representative and less biased. Asking friends or only current customers gives a biased view. Repeating the same small group does not fix this.","w":["The same small group gives the same bias. Repeating does not make the sample bigger or more varied.","Existing customers are not the whole target market. This sample would still be biased.","Closed questions change the question style, not the small, biased sample.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-072","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A firm launched a new snack and sales are weak. It wants detailed reasons why people dislike the taste. Which research method is most suitable?","o":["A government report on UK population","A focus group of target customers tasting the snack","Last year's sales figures for other snacks","An online article about snack trends"],"a":1,"x":"A focus group is primary research that gives in-depth qualitative opinions about the taste. The other options are secondary or internal data and cannot explain why these customers dislike this snack.","w":["Government population data is general secondary data and says nothing about taste.","","Sales figures show what sold, not why people dislike this snack's taste.","An article is general secondary data. It does not give reasons from this firm's customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-073","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A start-up plans to sell vegan protein bars. Which statement best explains why it should use both secondary and primary research?","o":["Primary research is always more reliable, so secondary research adds nothing","Secondary research is collected first-hand, so it is more accurate","Secondary gives cheap background; primary fills gaps about its own product","Using both methods guarantees that the product will succeed"],"a":2,"x":"Secondary research gives cheap, wide background such as market size. Primary research answers specific questions about the firm's own product and customers. The trap is thinking one method is always best or that research guarantees success.","w":["Secondary data is useful for cheap background, so it does add something. Primary is not always more reliable.","Secondary data is not collected first-hand. That describes primary research.","","Research reduces risk but cannot guarantee success. Other factors affect whether a product sells."]}
{"id":"gbu-074","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Why does a business use market segmentation?","o":["To sell exactly the same product to every customer","To aim its products and marketing at groups with similar needs","To get rid of all its competitors","To avoid doing any market research"],"a":1,"x":"Segmentation splits a market into groups of customers with similar needs. The business can then match products, prices and adverts to each group. The trap is thinking it removes the need for research or competitors.","w":["Segmentation does the opposite: it treats groups differently instead of selling one product to all.","","Segmentation does not remove competitors, who can still sell to the same segments.","Segmentation relies on research to find out what each group wants, so research is still needed."]}
{"id":"gbu-075","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A company advertises protein bars in gyms and running magazines. It aims at people who exercise regularly. Which type of segmentation is it mainly using?","o":["Age","Location","Lifestyle","Income"],"a":2,"x":"The group is defined by how people live: they exercise often. That is lifestyle segmentation. The trap is picking income or age. The company has not said how much people earn or how old they are.","w":["Nothing says the customers are a particular age, as people who exercise come from all age groups.","The bars are not aimed at people in one place, such as a region or town.","","Nothing is said about how much customers earn; the group is defined by how they live."]}
{"id":"gbu-076","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A new café owner uses a market map of local cafés (price and quality). She plans to open at mid price and high quality, where five cafés already sit. What does the map show her?","o":["She will face strong competition there and must stand out","Sales there are guaranteed because many cafés sell there","There are no competitors, as the position is a gap","Many cafés there must be failing, so demand is low"],"a":0,"x":"Five cafés at that position means it is not a gap. She would face strong competition. A market map shows where competitors are. It does not show sales or prove that demand is high or low.","w":["","Many competitors does not guarantee sales, because customers are shared between them.","A position with five cafés is crowded, not a gap, so this confuses a crowded spot with a gap.","A market map does not show profits or demand, so assuming many cafés means failure is wrong."]}
{"id":"gbu-077","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A sports brand makes separate trainers for runners, skaters and walkers, each with its own adverts. Which statement about segmentation is correct?","o":["It guarantees every customer in each segment will buy the trainers","It means the brand must charge every group exactly the same price","It replaces market research because the brand already knows its customers","It targets each group's needs but can raise costs for design and adverts"],"a":3,"x":"Segmentation lets a business match products and promotion to each group. The drawback is higher cost, because several products and adverts are needed. It never guarantees sales, and research is still needed.","w":["Targeting a segment makes sales more likely but never guarantees them, as customers still choose for themselves.","Segmentation allows different prices for different groups rather than forcing one price.","Research is still needed to find out what each segment wants, so segmentation does not replace it.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-078","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A business finds that a rival often delivers orders late. How should the business see this?","o":["As a weakness in its own business","As a rival's weakness it could use to win customers","As a strength of the rival","As something with no effect on its own decisions"],"a":1,"x":"Late delivery is a weakness of the rival. A business can use it by offering fast, reliable delivery. The trap is thinking a rival's problems have no effect on your own decisions.","w":["The weakness belongs to the rival, not to the business, so this mixes up whose weakness it is.","","Late delivery harms the rival's service, so it is not a strength. This misreads what a strength is.","Studying rivals is meant to shape decisions, so saying it has no effect misses the point of competitor analysis."]}
{"id":"gbu-079","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A new rival opens and sells very similar products to an existing café. What is the most likely effect on the existing café?","o":["Its costs will fall automatically","Demand for its products will definitely rise","It can now raise its prices without losing sales","It may lose some customers and need to improve its offer"],"a":3,"x":"More competition usually means customers have more choice. The existing café may lose sales and may need to improve quality, service or price. The trap is thinking competition helps existing businesses.","w":["A new rival does not change the café's costs, so this confuses competition with cost savings.","Customers can now choose the rival, so demand is more likely to fall than rise.","With more choice, higher prices are likely to push customers away, so this has the effect backwards.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-080","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A rival gym charges £20 a month but has old equipment and no classes. A new gym wants to use this weakness. Which decision does that best?","o":["Offer modern equipment and classes, even at a higher price","Charge £15 a month and offer the same old equipment","Buy cheap, old equipment to keep costs low","Open far from the rival, in an area with few possible customers"],"a":0,"x":"The rival's weaknesses are equipment and a lack of classes. Offering both targets customers who want more than the basics. The trap is thinking the only way to compete is to charge less.","w":["","Undercutting on price while matching the rival's weak equipment gives customers no new reason to switch.","Copying old equipment copies the rival's weakness rather than using it.","Few potential customers means few sales, so this ignores the rival's weakness and the market."]}
{"id":"gbu-081","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Five burger vans on one street sell almost identical burgers at £5. One van raises its price to £7 and changes nothing else. What is the most likely result?","o":["It will win customers, because people link higher prices with higher quality","Sales stay the same, because customers are loyal to one van","It will lose customers to the vans that still charge £5","The other four vans will raise their prices to £7 too"],"a":2,"x":"When products are very similar and rivals are close by, customers switch to the cheaper choice. A higher price only works if customers see extra value. The trap is assuming a higher price always signals quality.","w":["Nothing has improved, so customers have no reason to see the burger as higher quality.","There is no sign of strong loyalty, and with such similar burgers customers switch easily.","","Rivals gain customers by keeping prices low, so they have no reason to follow the increase."]}
{"id":"gbu-082","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A firm sells hand-made tables for £500, costing £400 each to make. A rival selling mass-produced tables cuts its prices by 20%. Which advice is best for the firm?","o":["Match the 20% cut, which keeps profit at £100 per table","Keep £500 and promote quality, as matching gives £0 profit","Match the cut, because price is all customers care about","Ignore the rival, because rivals' prices never affect sales"],"a":1,"x":"A 20% cut takes £100 off, so the price would be £400. That equals the cost, so profit would be £0. The firm should compete on its quality and craftsmanship. The trap is copying the rival's price cut.","w":["Matching the cut gives a price of £400, which equals the cost, so profit would be £0, not £100.","","Many customers choose hand-made goods for quality, so saying price is the only factor is false and would still give £0 profit.","Rivals' prices can affect a business's sales, so it should keep watching and respond with care."]}
{"id":"gbu-083","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A new business has just opened. Which aim is it most likely to have in its first year?","o":["Making the highest possible profit","Staying in business","Opening shops abroad","Becoming the market leader"],"a":1,"x":"Many new businesses have little money and few customers, so their first aim is survival. Big goals like market leadership or expanding abroad usually come later, once the business is stable.","w":["Maximum profit is unrealistic at the start. Confusing a long-term aim with a first-year aim is the slip.","","Expanding abroad needs lots of money and experience. It is a later growth aim, not a first-year one.","Market leader is a long-term aim needing a large share of sales. New firms must survive first."]}
{"id":"gbu-084","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of a social objective?","o":["Increasing sales by 8% this year","Paying off a loan within two years","Earning the owner a salary of £30,000","Giving 5% of profits to a local food bank"],"a":3,"x":"A social objective aims to benefit people or the community, not just the business. Giving profits to a food bank does this. Sales, loan and salary targets are financial objectives.","w":["Sales growth is a financial objective. It is about money, not helping the community.","Repaying a loan is a financial objective. Money targets are not social.","A salary is a financial goal for the owner. It does not benefit the community.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-085","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A sole trader runs a craft shop for personal satisfaction. A start-up has outside investors. Why are their aims likely to differ?","o":["Owners have different motives, and investors usually want growth and returns","Sole traders are not allowed to set aims for their business","Only start-ups with investors need to set financial aims","Their aims are the same because all businesses want maximum profit"],"a":0,"x":"Aims depend on who owns the business and why. The sole trader may value enjoyment and independence. Investors want their money to grow. The trap is thinking every business has the same aim.","w":["","Any business can set aims. Believing sole traders cannot is a false idea about who sets objectives.","Every business needs some financial aims, such as survival. Linking aims only to investors is the slip.","Not all owners chase maximum profit. Assuming one aim for everyone ignores personal satisfaction and other goals."]}
{"id":"gbu-087","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A new clothing firm pays its suppliers higher prices so workers are treated fairly. What is the likely effect on its objectives?","o":["It raises profit because higher prices mean lower costs","It helps a social objective but may reduce profit in the short run","It raises market share because the firm can cut its prices","It has no effect because social objectives are not real objectives"],"a":1,"x":"Paying suppliers more supports a social aim of fair treatment. But it raises costs, so profit may fall in the short run. The trap is thinking social and financial aims never conflict.","w":["Higher prices paid to suppliers mean higher costs, not lower. Mixing up costs and prices is the slip.","","Higher costs make it harder to cut prices. Assuming extra spending lowers selling prices is the slip.","Social objectives are real non-financial objectives. Many businesses set them, so saying they do not count is wrong."]}
{"id":"gbu-088","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A business borrows £12,000 for 2 years. The interest rate is 4% a year, and interest is worked out on the £12,000 each year. How much interest does it pay in total?","o":["£480","£960","£12,960","£96"],"a":1,"x":"Interest for one year = 4% of £12,000 = £480. Over 2 years that is £960. The trap is stopping at one year or giving the total repaid instead of the interest.","w":["That is only one year of interest; the loan lasts 2 years.","","That is the loan plus interest (£12,000 + £960), not the interest alone.","Decimal slip: used 0.4% instead of 4%, which gives £48 a year."]}
{"id":"gbu-089","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"On a break-even chart, the total cost line starts at £4,000 and meets the revenue line at 800 units and £8,000. What is the profit if 1,000 units are sold?","o":["£2,000","£6,000","£200","£1,000"],"a":3,"x":"Price = £8,000 ÷ 800 = £10. Variable cost = (£8,000 − £4,000) ÷ 800 = £5 a unit. At 1,000 units: £10,000 − (£4,000 + £5,000) = £1,000. The trap is taking off only the fixed costs, giving £6,000.","w":["Multiplied the 200-unit margin of safety by the £10 price; that is revenue, not profit.","Took fixed costs off revenue (£10,000 − £4,000) and forgot the variable costs.","That is the margin of safety in units, not a profit in pounds.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-090","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"A firm sells goods worth £800 in March. The customer agrees to pay in May. In which month does the £800 appear as a cash inflow in the cash-flow forecast?","o":["March, when the sale is made","May, when the money arrives","April, the month after the sale","Split equally between March and May"],"a":1,"x":"A cash-flow forecast records when money actually moves. The £800 arrives in May, so it is a May inflow. The trap is counting it when the sale is made.","w":["The sale is made in March, but no cash arrives yet; this confuses a sale with receiving cash.","","The money does not arrive in April; the forecast shows the month the cash is actually received.","Cash is recorded in full when it is received, not split across months."]}
{"id":"gbu-091","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"A small business is short of cash this month. Which action is most likely to help its cash flow?","o":["Order extra stock to get a bulk discount","Allow customers 60 days to pay instead of 30","Pay suppliers early to keep them happy","Negotiate longer credit terms with suppliers"],"a":3,"x":"Longer credit from suppliers means cash goes out later, which helps cash flow now. The traps are actions that make cash leave sooner or arrive later.","w":["Buying extra stock uses cash now and ties it up in unsold goods.","Giving customers longer to pay delays cash coming in, which makes the problem worse.","Paying early makes cash leave sooner, so it worsens the shortage.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-092","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"April: opening balance £500, inflows £3,000, outflows £3,400. May: inflows £2,600 and closing balance -£300. What are May's cash outflows?","o":["£3,000","£2,900","£3,400","£2,400"],"a":0,"x":"April closing = 500 + 3,000 - 3,400 = £100, so May opens at £100. Then 100 + 2,600 - outflows = -300, so outflows = £3,000. The trap is using the wrong opening balance.","w":["","Ignored May's opening balance of £100 and used only inflows and the closing balance.","Used April's opening balance (£500) instead of April's closing balance (£100).","Treated the closing balance as +£300 instead of -£300, a sign slip."]}
{"id":"gbu-093","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":3,"q":"On 1 January a firm has £1,000 cash. It pays £3,000 cash for goods and sells them for £5,000 on credit in January. The customer pays in March. What are January's profit and closing cash?","o":["Profit £2,000; closing cash £3,000","Profit -£3,000; closing cash -£2,000","Profit £2,000; closing cash -£2,000","Profit £5,000; closing cash £6,000"],"a":2,"x":"Profit on the sale is £5,000 - £3,000 = £2,000. But no cash has come in yet, so cash is 1,000 - 3,000 = -£2,000. Profit and cash are different things.","w":["Added the £2,000 profit to opening cash as if the customer had already paid.","Left the £5,000 sale out of profit because the cash had not arrived; profit counts the sale when made.","","Counted the £5,000 as cash received and as profit, ignoring the £3,000 cost and the credit delay."]}
{"id":"gbu-094","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which one of these is an internal source of finance for a business?","o":["A bank loan","Retained profit","Trade credit from a supplier","Money from a crowdfunding website"],"a":1,"x":"Retained profit is profit the business keeps and reinvests, so it comes from inside the firm. Loans, trade credit and crowdfunding all bring money in from outside, so they are external sources.","w":["A bank loan comes from a bank outside the business, so it is external; this mixes up 'long-term' with 'internal'.","","Trade credit comes from a supplier outside the business, so it is external; it is a short-term source, not an internal one.","Crowdfunding raises money from many outside people, so it is external; this confuses 'small amounts' with 'own money'."]}
{"id":"gbu-095","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which source of finance is available only to a limited company, and not to a sole trader?","o":["Personal savings","A bank overdraft","Trade credit","Share capital"],"a":3,"x":"Only limited companies can sell shares to raise share capital. Sole traders are not split into shares. Savings, overdrafts and trade credit can be used by any type of business.","w":["Sole traders often start with their own savings, so this is not limited to companies.","A bank can give an overdraft to a sole trader as well as to a company.","Suppliers can offer trade credit to any business, including sole traders.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-096","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A new firm accepts venture capital to help it grow fast. Which is a likely drawback for the owner?","o":["The owner may have to give up some ownership and control","The money must be repaid in full within a month","The firm must pay back the money with interest every week","The firm can only spend the money on short-term needs"],"a":0,"x":"Venture capital investors usually take a share of the business, so owners lose some ownership and control. It is long-term finance, not a short-term loan. The trap is treating it like a loan with fixed repayments.","w":["","Venture capital is long-term finance, not a one-month debt; this confuses it with a very short-term source.","Venture capital is not a loan with weekly repayments; investors take a share of the business instead.","Venture capital is long-term and used for growth, not just short-term needs; this mixes it up with an overdraft."]}
{"id":"gbu-097","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A games designer needs £20,000. She posts her idea on a website. Hundreds of people each give a small amount, and get a copy of the game as a reward. What is this source of finance?","o":["A bank overdraft","Retained profit","Crowdfunding","Trade credit"],"a":2,"x":"Crowdfunding raises money from many people, each giving a small amount, usually through a website. Overdrafts and trade credit come from banks or suppliers, and retained profit comes from the business itself.","w":["An overdraft comes from a bank, not from many people online; it is also short-term.","Retained profit is profit kept inside the business; a new idea with no sales has none.","","Trade credit means paying a supplier later, not receiving money from the public."]}
{"id":"gbu-098","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":3,"q":"A six-month-old firm has made no profit yet. It needs £30,000 for equipment. The owner wants to keep full ownership. Which source is most suitable?","o":["Share capital from new shareholders","A bank loan","Venture capital","Retained profit"],"a":1,"x":"A bank loan gives the money without giving away ownership. The firm must repay it with interest. Retained profit is not possible with no profit. Shares and venture capital both mean giving up part of the business.","w":["Selling shares gives new shareholders part ownership, which the owner wants to avoid.","","Venture capital investors normally take a share of the business, so the owner would lose full ownership.","Retained profit needs past profits, and this firm has made none, so it cannot fund the equipment."]}
{"id":"gbu-099","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What does limited liability mean for the owners of a business?","o":["They must repay all business debts using their own house and savings","They can only lose the money they put into the business","The business is only allowed to borrow a set amount of money","The business can only sell a set number of products each year"],"a":1,"x":"Limited liability means owners can lose only what they invested in the business. Their personal belongings are safe. The trap is thinking 'limited' refers to borrowing or sales, rather than the owners' responsibility for debts.","w":["This describes unlimited liability, which sole traders and partners have. It mixes up the two types.","","'Limited' here is about the owners' risk, not how much the business can borrow. This misreads the word.","'Limited' is not about sales or products. This misreads the term as a cap on trading."]}
{"id":"gbu-100","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage for a franchisee of running a franchise?","o":["They keep all of the profit the business makes","They can choose any products and prices they like","They pay no money to the franchisor to get started","They use a well-known brand and get help and training"],"a":3,"x":"A franchisee gets an established brand, a proven business model and support from the franchisor. The traps are thinking they keep all profit or have full freedom. They pay fees or royalties and must follow the franchisor's rules.","w":["Franchisees usually pay the franchisor a royalty from their sales or profit, so they do not keep it all.","The franchisor sets rules on products, prices and appearance. Freedom is limited, which is a disadvantage.","Franchisees normally pay an upfront fee to buy the franchise, so there is a start-up cost.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-101","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Priya and Sam run a partnership. It owes £30,000 but its assets are worth only £10,000. What is likely to happen?","o":["The £20,000 debt is cancelled because the business has closed","Only the business assets can be taken, so the debt stops at £10,000","They may have to pay the extra £20,000 from their personal assets","Creditors must accept the loss as the business is a separate legal person"],"a":2,"x":"Partners have unlimited liability. If the business cannot pay, they may have to use personal assets to cover the £20,000 shortfall. The trap is treating a partnership like a limited company, where owners' personal assets are protected.","w":["Debts are not cancelled when a business closes. Creditors can still claim, so this ignores unlimited liability.","This is the idea of limited liability. Partners are not protected in this way.","","A separate legal identity belongs to limited companies. Partnerships do not have it, so this confuses the types."]}
{"id":"gbu-102","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Which is a disadvantage of a private limited company compared with a sole trader?","o":["There is more legal paperwork to complete and more set-up costs","The owners risk their personal savings if the business fails","It is harder to raise money by selling shares to other people","All profits must be shared equally between all of the employees"],"a":0,"x":"A private limited company must be formally registered and must follow more legal rules, so it is more work and cost to set up. The trap is thinking owners lose personal savings. Limited liability protects them, and that is an advantage.","w":["","Shareholders have limited liability, so personal savings are protected. This mixes up limited and unlimited liability.","Selling shares to raise money is easier for a limited company. Sole traders cannot sell shares.","Profits are shared between shareholders, not equally between employees. This is an invented rule."]}
{"id":"gbu-103","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":3,"q":"A franchisee's monthly sales are £25,000. Costs before royalty are £17,000. The franchisor takes a royalty of 8% of sales. What is the franchisee's monthly profit?","o":["£8,000","£6,000","£7,360","£4,000"],"a":1,"x":"Royalty = 8% of £25,000 = £2,000. Profit = £25,000 − £17,000 − £2,000 = £6,000. The traps are forgetting the royalty or working out the royalty on profit instead of sales.","w":["This is sales minus costs only. It forgets to take off the royalty the franchisee pays.","","This takes 8% of the £8,000 profit instead of 8% of sales. The royalty is based on sales.","This takes off the £2,000 royalty twice. It should only be deducted once."]}
{"id":"gbu-104","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":1,"q":"Which business is most likely to choose its location mainly to be close to its raw materials?","o":["A hair salon in a shopping centre","A firm that makes bricks from heavy clay","An online shop selling phone cases","A café next to a railway station"],"a":1,"x":"Heavy, bulky materials cost a lot to move, so a brick maker sets up near its clay. Shops and cafés need to be near customers instead.","w":["A salon sells a service to customers, so it needs to be near them, not near materials.","","An online shop's main inputs are not bulky, and it does not rely on passing trade or local materials.","A café wants passing customers from the station; this is proximity to the market, not materials."]}
{"id":"gbu-105","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":1,"q":"An online-only company sells handmade candles across the UK. Why can it use cheap premises far from its customers?","o":["Customers buy through its website, so they do not need to visit","Online firms do not have to pay rent or business rates","Online firms never need to deliver goods to customers","Customers prefer to buy from firms in remote places"],"a":0,"x":"E-commerce means customers order online, so footfall does not matter. The firm can pick low-cost premises. The trap is thinking online firms need no premises or delivery.","w":["","Online firms still need a unit or warehouse and still pay rent, so this exaggerates the effect of the internet.","Goods still have to be posted or delivered; this confuses online ordering with online delivery.","Customers do not choose based on where the firm is; this is made up and not the real reason."]}
{"id":"gbu-106","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A new coffee shop opens on a street with five other coffee shops. Which is a likely benefit of being near its rivals?","o":["It will not need to set prices because rivals set them","Its rivals will have to close because it is there","It will pay lower rent than anywhere else","The street is known for coffee, so it attracts many customers"],"a":3,"x":"Clusters of similar businesses draw in customers who come to compare and choose. The trap is thinking rivals help with prices or rent. Busy areas often have higher rent.","w":["Every business sets its own prices; rivals do not set them for it, so this is a false idea.","A new entrant does not force rivals to close; this confuses competition with winning all the trade.","Busy, popular streets often have higher rent, so this wrongly links rivals to cheaper premises.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-107","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"Site A: rent £4,000, wages £12,000, transport £1,500 a month. Site B: rent £2,500, wages £13,000, transport £3,200 a month. Which site is cheaper, and by how much?","o":["Site B, by £1,500","Site B, by £1,200","Site A, by £1,200","Site A, by £1,700"],"a":2,"x":"Site A costs £17,500 and Site B costs £18,700. A is cheaper by £1,200. The trap is looking at only one cost, such as rent, instead of adding all the costs.","w":["This uses only the rent difference; adding wages and transport makes B the dearer site overall.","The gap is right but the wrong way round: B costs £18,700, more than A's £17,500.","","This is only the transport difference (£3,200 − £1,500). It ignores rent and wages."]}
{"id":"gbu-108","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm makes fresh sandwiches for shops, delivered each morning. It uses many hourly-paid staff. Which site is the best choice?","o":["A remote village with the lowest rent, 90 minutes from the shops","A town 15 minutes from the shops with plenty of local workers","A busy city-centre street with the highest rent, to attract passers-by","A port town, because sandwiches need imported materials"],"a":1,"x":"Fresh food must reach shops quickly, and the firm needs a large pool of workers. A town close to customers with local labour fits both. Lowest rent alone is a trap.","w":["Low rent is tempting, but a long journey harms freshness and raises transport costs, and the village has few workers.","","The firm sells to shops, not the public, so passing trade is no use and the high rent is wasted.","Nothing suggests the main materials are imported, so this invents a reason to locate at a port."]}
{"id":"gbu-109","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"A small firm launches a new snack. It sets a low price at first to win customers and gain market share. Which pricing strategy is this?","o":["Price skimming","Cost-plus pricing","Penetration pricing","Psychological pricing"],"a":2,"x":"Penetration pricing means a low price at launch to win market share fast. Do not mix it up with skimming, which starts high and lowers the price later.","w":["Skimming starts with a high price, so this mixes up the two launch strategies.","Cost-plus adds a mark-up to unit cost and is not about launching low to win share.","","Psychological pricing uses prices like £9.99 to seem cheaper, not a low launch price to gain share."]}
{"id":"gbu-110","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"A small bakery decides to sell its cakes through its own website and deliver them to homes. Which part of the marketing mix is this decision?","o":["Place","Promotion","Price","Product"],"a":0,"x":"Place is about how and where customers get the product, such as shops or online selling (e-tailing). Selling through a website is a place decision, not promotion.","w":["","Promotion tells customers about the product, but choosing a sales channel is a place decision.","Price is about how much customers pay, and choosing where to sell does not set it.","Product is about what is sold, such as design, not how customers get it."]}
{"id":"gbu-111","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A small firm makes a mug at a cost of £8 per unit. It uses cost-plus pricing with a mark-up of 50%. What is the selling price?","o":["£4","£12","£16","£8.50"],"a":1,"x":"50% of £8 is £4. Add this to the cost: £8 + £4 = £12. The trap is giving the mark-up alone, or doubling the cost.","w":["£4 is only the mark-up, so the slip is forgetting to add it to the £8 cost.","","£16 is a 100% mark-up, so the slip is treating 50% as doubling the cost.","£8.50 treats the mark-up as 50 pence instead of 50% of £8."]}
{"id":"gbu-112","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":3,"q":"A small business sells handmade luxury chocolates. Which marketing mix fits its aim of a high-quality image?","o":["Low price, plain packaging, sold in discount stores, money-off coupons","Premium price, gift box, sold in discount stores, 'cheapest around' adverts","Premium price, plastic bags, sold at a market stall, 'bargain' flyers","Premium price, gift box, sold in independent delis, food magazine adverts"],"a":3,"x":"The four Ps must work together and match the image. A premium price, quality packaging, upmarket outlets and a food magazine all say high quality. The trap is choosing a mix where one P clashes.","w":["Low price, plain packaging and coupons all suggest a budget product, clashing with a luxury image.","Price and box fit luxury, but discount stores and 'cheapest' adverts clash with it.","A premium price clashes with plastic bags, a market stall and bargain flyers.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-113","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A small firm launches a unique gadget that rivals cannot yet copy. It uses price skimming. Why might this suit the firm?","o":["Customers are very price-sensitive and compare many rivals","The firm wants to gain a large market share as quickly as possible","Some early customers will pay a high price for a new, unique product","Each item costs so little to make that it must be sold cheaply"],"a":2,"x":"Skimming sets a high price at launch. It works when a product is new and unique, so early buyers will pay more. Fast market share is the aim of penetration pricing.","w":["Price-sensitive customers with many rivals suit low prices, not a high skimming price.","Gaining quick market share is the aim of penetration pricing, not skimming.","","Low cost does not force a low price, as firms can charge what customers will pay."]}
{"id":"gbu-114","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":1,"q":"How does writing a business plan help a new business owner reduce risk?","o":["It guarantees that enough customers will buy the product","It makes the owner check costs and the market before spending money","It stops competitors from opening nearby","It means the owner no longer needs a cash-flow forecast"],"a":1,"x":"Planning makes the owner research the market and work out costs before money is spent, so problems show up early. A plan cannot guarantee sales or block competitors. Risk is reduced, not removed.","w":["A plan cannot guarantee sales; thinking it can confuses planning with certainty.","","A plan cannot stop competitors opening; it only helps the owner prepare for them.","The cash-flow forecast is part of the plan, so it is still needed."]}
{"id":"gbu-115","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":1,"q":"Which part of a business plan shows the money expected to come in and go out each month?","o":["The cash-flow forecast","The profit forecast","The business aims and objectives","The target market description"],"a":0,"x":"A cash-flow forecast shows the timing of cash coming in and going out. A profit forecast only compares total revenue with total costs. It does not show when the cash actually moves.","w":["","A profit forecast shows revenue minus costs, not the monthly timing of cash, so this mixes up profit and cash.","Aims and objectives set out what the owner wants to achieve, not cash movements.","The target market describes the customers and says nothing about cash."]}
{"id":"gbu-116","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":2,"q":"A plan says: \"Our customers will be students aged 16 to 21 who live within 2 km of the shop.\" Which component of the business plan is this?","o":["Sources of finance","Location","Competitor analysis","Target market"],"a":3,"x":"The sentence describes who the customers are: their age and type. That is the target market. The 2 km distance is a trap: it describes where customers live, not where the business will be sited.","w":["Sources of finance explains where start-up money comes from, but the sentence mentions no money.","The 2 km distance is a trap, because the sentence describes customers, not the shop's own site.","Competitor analysis looks at rival businesses, but the sentence only describes customers.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-117","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":2,"q":"A start-up needs equipment £18,000, a van £14,000, rent £6,000 and stock £7,000. The owner has £15,000 savings and family give £6,000. How much must the bank lend?","o":["£39,000","£30,000","£24,000","£21,000"],"a":2,"x":"Total start-up costs are £18,000 + £14,000 + £6,000 + £7,000 = £45,000. The owner and family provide £21,000. The bank must lend £45,000 − £21,000 = £24,000. The trap is forgetting one source.","w":["This only subtracts the family's £6,000 and ignores the owner's savings.","This only subtracts the owner's £15,000 and ignores the family's money.","","£21,000 is the money already raised, not the amount still needed from the bank."]}
{"id":"gbu-118","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":3,"q":"A cash-flow forecast in a new business's plan shows a negative closing balance in months 2, 3 and 4. What does this show, and what should the plan then include?","o":["Losses in those months, so the plan should be dropped","Cash will run short, so the plan should show how it will be financed","A mistake, so the balances should be changed to zero","High profit in those months, so no finance is needed"],"a":1,"x":"A negative closing balance means the business will run out of cash in those months. A good plan shows how to cover the gap, for example with an overdraft. Cash shortage is not the same as making a loss.","w":["This confuses cash with profit, and a cash shortage can be covered with finance, so the plan need not be dropped.","","A negative balance is a possible real outcome, and changing it to zero hides the problem from lenders.","A negative cash balance does not mean high profit; it means the business will not have enough cash."]}
{"id":"gbu-119","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is the main aim of a supplier to a business?","o":["Low prices for the finished products","Regular orders and prompt payment","A high dividend each year","Safe working conditions in the factory"],"a":1,"x":"Suppliers sell goods or services to the business. They want repeat orders and to be paid on time. Low prices suit customers, dividends suit shareholders and safe conditions suit employees.","w":["Low prices for finished products are what customers want, not suppliers.","","Dividends are paid to shareholders; suppliers are paid for the goods they sell.","Safe working conditions are an aim of employees, not of suppliers outside the business."]}
{"id":"gbu-120","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"A pressure group is unhappy about pollution from a factory. Which action is it most likely to take?","o":["Set the prices of the factory's products","Pay the wages of the factory's staff","Decide how much dividend is paid","Run a campaign to get media attention"],"a":3,"x":"Pressure groups try to influence a business by campaigning, using publicity and social media. They do not own or run the business, so they cannot set prices, wages or dividends.","w":["Prices are set by the business; a pressure group does not manage the firm.","Wages are paid by the employer; a pressure group is not part of the business.","Dividends are decided by the directors, not by an outside pressure group.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-121","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A shop stops buying from a local farm and switches to a cheaper overseas supplier. Which stakeholder is most directly harmed?","o":["The local farm that loses the orders","The shop's owners, as costs will rise","The shop's customers, who will pay more","The government, as the shop stops paying tax"],"a":0,"x":"The farm loses sales and income because it no longer supplies the shop. A cheaper supplier should cut costs, so owners are more likely to gain. Do not assume every change harms every group.","w":["","Costs should fall with a cheaper supplier, so owners are unlikely to be harmed; this reverses the effect.","A cheaper supplier does not make customers pay more; prices may even fall.","The shop still pays its taxes, so the government is not directly harmed."]}
{"id":"gbu-122","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm has £200,000 of profit to share out. It pays 40% as dividends. Staff then want a £50,000 bonus from what remains. How much is left?","o":["£150,000","£30,000","£70,000","£120,000"],"a":2,"x":"40% of £200,000 is £80,000 in dividends, leaving £120,000. A £50,000 bonus then leaves £70,000. The trap is skipping a step or subtracting from the dividend instead of the remaining profit.","w":["This ignores the dividend and only takes off the £50,000 bonus.","This takes the bonus from the £80,000 dividend instead of the remaining profit.","","This stops after the dividend and forgets to take off the bonus."]}
{"id":"gbu-123","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":3,"q":"A clothes retailer switches to ethically sourced cotton, which costs more. Which statement about its stakeholders is most accurate?","o":["Customers all gain, because the price of its clothes will fall","Pressure groups may approve, but shareholders may fear lower profit","Its old cotton suppliers gain, because they will get more orders","The government will pay the extra cost, so no stakeholder loses"],"a":1,"x":"Ethical sourcing may please pressure groups and some customers. Higher costs can cut profit unless prices rise, so shareholders may be concerned. This is a clash between stakeholders' aims.","w":["Ethical cotton costs more, so prices are more likely to rise than fall.","","The old suppliers lose orders when the retailer changes supplier, so they do not gain.","The business pays its own costs; the government does not pay for the cotton."]}
{"id":"gbu-124","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A shop that only sells in town starts selling through its own website too. Which is a benefit of this change?","o":["It will have no staff or delivery costs to pay","It can reach customers far away at any time of day","Customers can try the goods before they buy them","It is safe from hackers and data theft"],"a":1,"x":"A website lets a business sell beyond its local area, 24 hours a day. The trap is thinking online selling removes costs or risks. It does not.","w":["Online selling still needs staff and adds delivery costs, so technology does not remove all costs.","","Customers cannot touch or try goods online, which is a drawback of online selling, not a benefit.","Websites can be hacked, so online selling adds security risks and costs rather than removing them."]}
{"id":"gbu-125","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A café starts accepting contactless and mobile phone payments. Which is the most likely benefit to the café?","o":["Customers pay faster, so queues are shorter","The café pays no charges to banks or card companies","The café no longer needs to record its sales","No payment can ever be fraudulent"],"a":0,"x":"Contactless and mobile payments speed up each sale, so queues shrink and more customers can be served. The trap is assuming it is free or risk-free. Card fees and fraud still exist.","w":["","Card providers usually charge businesses a fee on each payment, so digital payments are not free.","Sales must still be recorded for accounts and tax, because technology changes how sales are recorded, not whether.","Fraud and stolen cards still happen, so technology does not make payments completely safe."]}
{"id":"gbu-126","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A small bakery in Bolton wants to reach local people aged 16 to 25 at low cost. Which method of promotion is the most suitable?","o":["A prime-time national TV advert","A full-page advert in a national newspaper","Targeted posts and offers on social media","Leaflets posted to every home in the UK"],"a":2,"x":"Social media is cheap and can be targeted by age and area, so it suits a small local business. The trap is choosing wide-reach methods that cost far too much and mostly reach the wrong people.","w":["National TV is very expensive and mostly reaches people outside Bolton, so it is neither low cost nor targeted.","A national newspaper advert costs a lot and most of its readers live far from the bakery.","","Posting to every UK home is costly and wasteful, because most people reached are not local customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-127","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A clothing retailer closes six high-street shops and sells only online. Which of its costs is most likely to fall?","o":["Delivery and packaging costs","Website running and security costs","Handling of returned items","Rent and rates on shop premises"],"a":3,"x":"Closing shops cuts rent and rates. The trap is forgetting that online selling adds new costs: delivery, packaging, returns, and website running and security.","w":["Online orders must be posted to customers, so delivery and packaging costs rise, not fall.","A busier website needs more hosting, upkeep and security, so these costs rise when selling only online.","Online buyers often send items back, so the cost of handling returns usually rises.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-128","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":3,"q":"A small online shop sells trainers. A price comparison website now shows its prices next to those of its rivals. What is the most likely effect on the shop?","o":["Customers find comparing harder, so it can raise prices","Customers stop caring about price and choose on brand only","It must keep prices competitive, which may cut its profit margin","Its rivals' costs fall, so its own costs fall as well"],"a":2,"x":"Comparison sites make prices easy to see, so customers can quickly switch to cheaper rivals. The shop may have to cut prices, reducing profit per sale. The trap is thinking technology only helps the seller.","w":["Comparison sites make comparing easier, so raising prices would risk losing sales to cheaper rivals.","Easy comparison makes customers more price-aware, not less, so price matters more.","","Rivals' costs do not set this shop's costs, so this confuses competitors' costs with its own."]}
{"id":"gbu-129","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A firm refuses to interview a woman for a job because she is 58. Which law is the firm most likely to have broken?","o":["Consumer Rights Act 2015","Health and Safety at Work Act 1974","Equality Act 2010","National Minimum Wage rules"],"a":2,"x":"The Equality Act 2010 stops employers treating people unfairly because of a protected characteristic, such as age. The trap is picking a law about goods, safety or pay, which do not cover hiring decisions.","w":["This covers goods sold to customers, not hiring, so it mixes up consumer law with employment law.","This covers safe working conditions, and refusing an interview because of age is not a safety issue.","","This sets the lowest legal pay, so it confuses pay law with discrimination law about who gets interviewed."]}
{"id":"gbu-130","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A shop employs 8 workers. Each works 25 hours a week. A rise in the minimum wage adds £0.60 to every hourly rate. What is the extra wage cost per week?","o":["£15","£120","£4.80","£33.60"],"a":1,"x":"Total hours are 8 × 25 = 200. Then 200 × £0.60 = £120 a week. The trap is working out the cost for one worker only, or forgetting to use the hours.","w":["This is 25 × £0.60, the cost for one worker only, forgetting there are 8 workers.","","This is 8 × £0.60, which forgets to multiply by the 25 hours each worker does.","This adds 8 + 25 + 0.60 instead of multiplying the numbers together."]}
{"id":"gbu-131","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A website describes a sofa as 'leather'. The sofa arrives covered in fabric. It is comfortable and works well. Which part of consumer law has the seller broken?","o":["Goods must be as described","Goods must be of satisfactory quality","Goods must be fit for purpose","No law is broken because the sofa can be used"],"a":0,"x":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 says goods must match their description. A fabric sofa is not leather, so it is not as described. The trap is thinking the sofa works, so no rule is broken.","w":["","The sofa is comfortable and works well, so its quality is not the problem here.","The sofa can still be sat on, so it is fit for purpose; the fault is the false description.","Working goods must still match their description, so being usable does not mean the law is met."]}
{"id":"gbu-132","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A café ignores health and safety law. It leaves a wet floor with no warning sign and a worker breaks an arm. What is a likely result for the café?","o":["The worker must pay for their own treatment","The law does not apply because the café is small","Customers must pay a fine instead of the café","It may have to pay compensation and could be fined"],"a":3,"x":"Employers must keep workers safe. If they fail, they can face fines, compensation claims and bad publicity. The trap is thinking small businesses are exempt from health and safety law.","w":["The employer has the duty to keep workers safe, so the injured worker is not the one who pays.","Health and safety law applies to all employers whatever their size, so small firms are not exempt.","Customers never pay fines for a business's failure because the legal duty is on the employer.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-133","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":3,"q":"Following employment law raises a small firm's costs. Which is the best reason the firm should still follow it?","o":["Laws only apply to firms that are making a profit","Following the law means rivals cannot compete","Fines, compensation and a poor reputation could cost more than complying","Fines for breaking the law are paid by the government"],"a":2,"x":"Breaking the law can lead to fines, compensation claims, lost staff and customers, and bad publicity. These can cost more than compliance. The trap is thinking only the short-term cost of complying matters.","w":["Laws apply to all firms whether or not they make a profit.","Rivals must follow the same laws, so complying does not stop them competing.","","The business that breaks the law pays its own fines, not the government."]}
{"id":"gbu-134","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":1,"q":"The Bank of England raises interest rates. A business has a bank loan on a variable rate. What is the most likely effect on the business?","o":["Its loan repayments fall","Its loan repayments stay the same","Its loan repayments are likely to rise","It must repay the whole loan at once"],"a":2,"x":"A variable rate follows the Bank of England rate. When interest rates rise, borrowing costs rise, so repayments go up. This cuts the firm's profit unless it raises prices.","w":["Reversed: higher rates make borrowing dearer, so repayments rise, not fall.","A variable rate changes when interest rates change, so repayments will not stay the same.","","A rate rise does not make a bank demand full repayment. It only raises the cost of borrowing."]}
{"id":"gbu-135","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":1,"q":"A company pays corporation tax to the government. What is corporation tax charged on?","o":["The profits made by a company","The goods and services a company sells","The wages a company pays to workers","The goods a company imports"],"a":0,"x":"Corporation tax is paid on company profits. Do not mix it up with VAT, which is added to the price of goods sold, or income tax, which is paid by people on their earnings.","w":["","This describes VAT, which is added to the selling price. Corporation tax is on profit.","Wages are taxed through income tax and National Insurance, not corporation tax.","Taxes on imports are tariffs or duties, not corporation tax."]}
{"id":"gbu-136","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A firm sells fitted kitchens. Most customers borrow money to pay for them. Interest rates fall. What is the likely effect on the firm?","o":["Demand falls because saving is more attractive","Demand falls because the firm's costs rise","Demand is unaffected because kitchens are a need","Demand is likely to rise because borrowing is cheaper"],"a":3,"x":"Lower interest rates make loans cheaper and saving less rewarding. Customers are more willing to borrow and spend on big items, so demand is likely to rise. The trap is thinking that lower rates reduce spending.","w":["Saving is less attractive when rates fall, not more. People are more likely to borrow and spend.","Lower rates cut the firm's own borrowing costs. They do not raise them.","Kitchens can be delayed, so demand is affected by the cost of borrowing.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-137","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A UK firm buys parts costing 5,000 US dollars. The rate falls from £1 = 1.25 dollars to £1 = 1.00 dollar. What happens to the cost of the parts in pounds?","o":["It falls by £1,000","It rises by £1,000","It rises by £1,250","It falls by £250"],"a":1,"x":"At 1.25 dollars to the pound, the parts cost £4,000 (5,000 ÷ 1.25). At 1.00 they cost £5,000. The weaker pound makes imports dearer, so the cost rises by £1,000.","w":["Reversed: a weaker pound buys fewer dollars, so imports cost more pounds, not fewer.","","£1,250 comes from multiplying 5,000 by the 0.25 change. You must divide by the exchange rate.","£250 is just the change in the rate. It is not the change in the cost, and the direction is wrong."]}
{"id":"gbu-138","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":3,"q":"A firm makes £200,000 profit before tax. The government cuts corporation tax from 25% to 20%. How much more profit does the firm keep after tax?","o":["£5,000","£40,000","£10,000","£50,000"],"a":2,"x":"The tax falls from £50,000 (25% of £200,000) to £40,000 (20%). The firm keeps £10,000 more. The trap is using the tax rate or the tax bill instead of the difference.","w":["£5,000 is 5% of only £100,000. The 5-point cut must be applied to the full £200,000.","£40,000 is the tax bill at 20%, not the extra profit kept.","","£50,000 is the old tax bill at 25%, not the extra profit kept."]}
{"id":"gbu-139","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":1,"q":"The pound rises in value against the euro. A UK firm sells goods to Spain and keeps its prices in pounds the same. What is the most likely effect?","o":["Its goods become cheaper for Spanish buyers, so sales rise","Its goods cost Spanish buyers the same, so nothing changes","Its goods become more expensive for Spanish buyers, so sales may fall","Its UK wage costs rise because of the exchange rate"],"a":2,"x":"A stronger pound means Spanish buyers need more euros to pay the same pound price. Exports become dearer abroad, so sales may fall. The trap is thinking a stronger pound makes exports cheaper.","w":["Wrong direction: a stronger pound makes UK exports dearer abroad, not cheaper.","Spanish buyers pay in euros, so the same pound price costs them more euros after the change.","","Exchange rates do not directly change UK wages. This confuses exchange rates with the minimum wage or inflation."]}
{"id":"gbu-140","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":1,"q":"A garden centre has a large bank loan with a variable interest rate. The Bank of England raises interest rates. What is the most likely effect on the garden centre?","o":["Loan repayments rise, so costs go up and profit may fall","Loan repayments fall, so costs go down and profit rises","Loan repayments stay the same because the loan size is fixed","Customers borrow more, so sales of plants and tools rise"],"a":0,"x":"A variable rate moves with interest rates, so repayments rise. Higher costs can cut profit. Higher rates also make customers spend less, not more. The trap is mixing up higher and lower rates.","w":["","Repayments rise, not fall, when interest rates go up. This reverses the effect of a rate rise.","The loan size is fixed but the interest charged is variable, so repayments change.","Higher rates make borrowing dearer, so customers usually spend less, not more."]}
{"id":"gbu-141","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":2,"q":"A café pays all its staff the minimum wage. Its weekly wage bill is £8,000. New legislation raises the minimum wage by 6%. What is the new weekly wage bill?","o":["£480","£7,520","£8,048","£8,480"],"a":3,"x":"6% of £8,000 is £480. The new bill is £8,000 + £480 = £8,480. The trap is giving only the increase, or taking the 6% off instead of adding it.","w":["£480 is only the increase. The question asks for the new total wage bill.","This takes 6% off the bill. A rise in the minimum wage increases costs.","£8,048 comes from finding 0.6% instead of 6%, a decimal slip.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-142","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":2,"q":"The economy goes into a recession. Many people lose jobs and household incomes fall. Which business is most likely to keep its sales up?","o":["A company selling luxury cruise holidays","A discount supermarket selling low-price own-brand food","A garage selling expensive new sports cars","A shop selling designer furniture"],"a":1,"x":"In a recession, people have less to spend and look for cheaper goods. Budget food sellers can keep or gain sales. Luxury items are bought less. The trap is thinking all businesses are hit equally.","w":["Luxury holidays are often cut first when incomes fall, so sales would likely drop.","","Expensive cars are costly, so many buyers delay them when incomes fall.","Designer furniture is a luxury and is often put off when incomes fall."]}
{"id":"gbu-143","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":3,"q":"A UK firm buys stock costing €6,000. At £1 = €1.20 it pays £5,000. The pound then falls to £1 = €1.00. How does the cost of the stock in pounds change?","o":["It falls by £1,000","It rises by £200","It rises by £1,000","It rises by £1,200"],"a":2,"x":"At £1 = €1.00 the stock costs £6,000 (€6,000 ÷ 1.00). Before it cost £5,000, so the cost rises by £1,000. The trap is thinking a weaker pound makes imports cheaper.","w":["A weaker pound makes imports dearer, not cheaper. This reverses the effect.","£200 wrongly treats the €0.20 rate change as pounds, not the change in cost.","","£1,200 comes from multiplying €6,000 by the 0.20 rate change instead of dividing by the new rate."]}
{"id":"gbu-144","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of organic (internal) growth for a business?","o":["Buying all the shares of a rival to take it over","Joining with another firm to form one new company","Launching a new product range paid for from its own profits","Merging with a supplier to share the costs"],"a":2,"x":"Organic growth comes from inside the business, for example new products or new markets. Takeovers and mergers are external growth because they involve another business.","w":["This is a takeover, which is external growth, not growth from inside the business.","This is a merger, which is external growth, because it involves another business.","","A merger with a supplier is external growth; the slip is thinking any growth plan counts as organic."]}
{"id":"gbu-145","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes a merger?","o":["Two businesses agree to join together to form one business","One business buys enough shares to take control of another","A business grows by selling more of its own products","A business sells shares to the public for the first time"],"a":0,"x":"A merger is when two firms agree to combine. The trap is mixing it up with a takeover, where one firm buys control of another and the target may not agree.","w":["","This describes a takeover, where one firm gains control of another; the slip is mixing up merger and takeover.","This is organic growth from within the business, not a merger with another firm.","This is becoming a plc by floating shares, not joining two businesses together."]}
{"id":"gbu-146","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm wants £80,000 for new machines. It will not take on debt and will not give anyone a share of ownership. Which source of finance fits?","o":["A bank loan","Selling new shares","Venture capital","Retained profit"],"a":3,"x":"Retained profit is money the firm has already earned and kept. It has no interest and no new owners. Loans create debt, while shares and venture capital give others a stake.","w":["A loan means debt and interest, which the firm wants to avoid.","Selling new shares gives new owners a share of the business, which the firm does not want.","Venture capital investors take a share of ownership, so the slip is thinking it is free money.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-147","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A UK café chain wants to enter a new region quickly. Which is the main advantage of taking over a local café firm rather than opening new cafés itself?","o":["It is always cheaper than organic growth","It gains local customers and market knowledge straight away","It avoids any need to combine two sets of staff","It lets the local firm's owners keep control of decisions"],"a":1,"x":"A takeover gives instant market share, customers and local knowledge. It is usually expensive, and merging staff and systems can be difficult. The trap is thinking external growth is cheap and easy.","w":["Takeovers are often costly because the other firm must be bought, so organic growth is usually cheaper.","","Combining staff, systems and cultures is a common problem after a takeover, not something avoided.","In a takeover the buying firm gains control, so the old owners lose their decision-making power."]}
{"id":"gbu-148","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":3,"q":"An owner holds 100% of a private company's shares. To become a plc, 55% of the shares are sold to the public. What is a key risk for the owner?","o":["They now hold 45%, so other shareholders together could outvote them","They now have unlimited liability for the company's debts","They must pay all profits out to shareholders as dividends","The company can no longer borrow money from banks"],"a":0,"x":"After selling 55%, the owner has 100% − 55% = 45%. Other shareholders hold a majority and could outvote them or take over. Limited liability stays. The trap is confusing loss of control with loss of limited liability.","w":["","Plc shareholders keep limited liability, so the owner is not personally liable for company debts.","Dividends are decided by the directors, so paying out all profits is not required.","A plc can still borrow from banks, and often finds it easier because it is larger."]}
{"id":"gbu-149","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which aim is a brand new business most likely to have in its first year of trading?","o":["Becoming the market leader","Expanding into other countries","Surviving and covering its costs","Paying large dividends to shareholders"],"a":2,"x":"New businesses usually have little money and few customers. Their first aim is survival. Aims such as growth or market leadership tend to come later. The trap is picking an ambitious aim too early.","w":["Market leadership needs a large market share, which a new firm will not have yet.","Selling abroad takes money and experience a start-up lacks. This assumes the firm is already established.","","Dividends are paid from profit to shareholders. A new firm is unlikely to have spare profit yet."]}
{"id":"gbu-150","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"In a recession, customers spend less and a café chain's sales fall. Which change in aim is most likely?","o":["Moving from survival to opening new cafés quickly","Moving from profit to ignoring costs","Moving from a small to a large market share target","Moving from growth to survival"],"a":3,"x":"A recession is a change in market conditions. Falling sales squeeze cash and profit, so firms often switch from growth to survival. The trap is thinking firms keep expanding when demand falls.","w":["Rapid expansion needs spare money. A firm with falling sales is unlikely to do this, so this reverses the logic.","Falling sales make costs more important to control, not less. Ignoring costs would risk the firm's survival.","A bigger market share target is a growth aim. Weak demand makes this harder, not more likely.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-151","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of a business changing its aims for an internal reason?","o":["A new owner wants the firm to put the environment before profit","A rival launches a cheaper product","Parliament raises the minimum wage","A new app lets customers order by phone"],"a":0,"x":"Internal reasons come from inside the business, such as new owners or managers with different priorities. A rival, a new law and new technology are all outside causes. The trap is mixing up inside and outside causes.","w":["","A rival's actions are a change in market conditions, which is external.","A change in the minimum wage is legislation, which is external.","A new app is a change in technology, which is an external cause."]}
{"id":"gbu-152","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A sole trader's firm becomes a private limited company with outside shareholders. Why might its main aim move towards profit and growth?","o":["Shareholders do not mind whether they get a return","Shareholders expect a return, so the owners must increase profit","Limited companies do not pay any tax on profit","Limited companies must grow by law each year"],"a":1,"x":"Outside shareholders invest to earn dividends and rising share value. This puts pressure on the owners to raise profit and grow. Wrongly assuming a company has no such pressure is the trap.","w":["Shareholders invest to get a return, so they do care. This misreads why people buy shares.","","Limited companies pay Corporation Tax on profits. Tax is not the reason for the change in aim.","No law forces limited companies to grow. This invents a rule that does not exist."]}
{"id":"gbu-153","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm has £200,000 sales in a £1 million market (20% market share). The market grows to £1.2 million and the firm now aims for 25% market share. What sales does it need?","o":["£250,000","£240,000","£300,000","£210,000"],"a":2,"x":"The new objective applies to the new market size. 25% of £1,200,000 is £300,000. The trap is using the old market size, or keeping the old 20% share.","w":["This is 25% of the old £1 million market. It ignores that the market has grown.","This is 20% of £1.2 million. It keeps the old market share instead of the new 25% aim.","","This adds 5% to current sales instead of working out 25% of the new market."]}
{"id":"gbu-154","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What is a trade bloc?","o":["A firm that has factories and shops in many countries","A group of countries that agree to trade with few or no trade barriers","A group of countries that put tariffs on each other's goods","A tax on goods that are brought into a country"],"a":1,"x":"A trade bloc, such as the EU, is a group of countries that trade freely with each other, often with no tariffs. A firm in many countries is a multinational. A tax on imports is a tariff.","w":["This describes a multinational, not a group of countries.","","The opposite is true: members of a trade bloc cut barriers such as tariffs between them.","This is a tariff, which is a barrier to trade, not a trade bloc."]}
{"id":"gbu-155","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a benefit to a UK business of starting to export its goods?","o":["It no longer has to face any competitors","It never has to pay any delivery costs","It avoids all tariffs in other countries","It can reach more customers, so sales can grow"],"a":3,"x":"Exporting gives access to a bigger market, so sales and profit can rise. The trap is thinking exporting removes problems. Competition, transport costs and tariffs can all still apply abroad.","w":["Overseas markets have their own competitors, so competition does not disappear.","Exporting usually adds transport and delivery costs, so this is wrong.","Other countries may charge tariffs on imports, so exporters can still face them.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-156","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A large multinational opens a supermarket in a small town. Why might local independent shops struggle to compete with it?","o":["It can often charge lower prices because it buys in bulk","Local shops must pay tariffs on all the goods they sell","It always sells better quality goods than local shops","Local shops are banned from selling the same products"],"a":0,"x":"Multinationals are large, so they buy in bulk and gain economies of scale. This lets them lower prices. The trap is assuming they are better quality or that local shops face extra tariffs.","w":["","Tariffs apply to imports crossing borders. Local shops do not pay them just because a multinational arrives.","Size does not guarantee better quality. This is a false assumption.","Nothing stops local shops selling similar products. Competition rules allow this."]}
{"id":"gbu-157","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A UK shop imports 200 coats from a country outside a trade bloc. Each coat costs £50. A 20% tariff is charged on the price. What is the total tariff paid?","o":["£10","£4,000","£2,000","£12,000"],"a":2,"x":"20% of £50 is £10 per coat. £10 × 200 = £2,000. The traps are stopping at the tariff per coat, or giving the total cost including the tariff.","w":["This is the tariff on one coat only. It was not multiplied by 200.","This uses 20 × 200, treating 20% as £20 per coat.","","This is the total cost of coats plus tariff (£10,000 + £2,000), not the tariff alone."]}
{"id":"gbu-158","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A UK firm sells bikes in Country X for £400. Country X adds a 10% tariff on imported bikes. Local bikes cost £420. Assume shoppers pay the tariff in the price. What is the most likely result?","o":["The UK firm receives the extra £40 as added income","The UK bike now costs more than the local bike, so UK sales will probably fall","The tariff makes the UK bike cheaper in Country X, so sales rise","UK taxpayers pay the tariff, so the shop price stays at £400"],"a":1,"x":"10% of £400 is £40, so the UK bike costs £440. That is more than the £420 local bike, so demand for it will probably fall. The trap is thinking the exporter gains the tariff. It goes to Country X's government.","w":["Tariff money goes to the government of Country X, not to the exporting firm.","","A tariff raises the price of imports. It does not make them cheaper.","Tariffs are paid on goods entering Country X, and they raise the price there. UK taxpayers do not pay them."]}
{"id":"gbu-159","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":1,"q":"What does it mean to say that a business decision is ethical?","o":["It is the cheapest choice available to the business","It is judged to be morally right and fair to others","It only meets the minimum the law demands","It gives shareholders the highest possible dividend"],"a":1,"x":"An ethical decision is based on what is seen as morally right and fair, such as how workers and suppliers are treated. The trap is confusing ethical with legal or with cheapest. Something can be legal but still unethical.","w":["Cheapest is about cost, and a low-cost choice can be unfair to workers or suppliers.","","Confusing ethical with legal is the slip, because a legal choice can still be unfair.","Maximising dividends is about owners' profit and ignores fairness to other stakeholders."]}
{"id":"gbu-160","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of a business acting sustainably?","o":["Buying the cheapest raw materials whatever their source","Using up a local resource quickly to boost this year's profit","Dumping waste and paying a small fine","A paper firm planting new trees to replace those it cuts down"],"a":3,"x":"Sustainability means using resources so that they are still available for future generations. Replacing trees does this. The trap is thinking only of short-term profit, which can use up resources.","w":["Ignoring the source can harm the environment and does not protect resources for the future.","Using a resource up quickly is short-term thinking that leaves less for the future.","Paying a fine does not stop dumped waste from harming the environment.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-161","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A firm switches to recycled packaging. It costs 8p more per unit. The firm sells 50,000 units a year. What is the extra cost of packaging each year?","o":["£4,000","£400","£40,000","£6,250"],"a":0,"x":"Extra cost = 8p × 50,000 = 400,000p. Divide by 100 to change pence to pounds: £4,000. The trap is a pence-to-pounds slip, which gives £400 or £40,000.","w":["","This divides 400,000p by 1,000 instead of 100 when converting pence to pounds.","This divides 400,000p by 10 instead of 100, a pence-to-pounds conversion slip.","This divides 50,000 by 8 instead of multiplying 8p by 50,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-162","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A clothing firm pays its overseas suppliers a fair price instead of the lowest price available. Which trade-off is it accepting?","o":["Higher profit now, in return for a damaged reputation","Lower costs now, in return for fewer customers","Lower profit margins now, in return for a better reputation","Higher costs now, in return for guaranteed higher sales"],"a":2,"x":"Paying suppliers fairly raises costs, so profit margins may fall. In return the firm may gain a better reputation and loyal customers. The trap is thinking that ethical choices guarantee higher sales.","w":["This is the wrong way round, since paying fairly raises costs and tends to help reputation.","Fair prices raise costs rather than lower them, and acting ethically is unlikely to lose customers.","","Costs do rise, but higher sales are never guaranteed by ethical choices."]}
{"id":"gbu-163","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A café makes 40p profit per drink and sells 90,000 a year. It changes to compostable cups costing 10p more. It raises its price by 6p. How many extra drinks must it sell to keep total profit the same?","o":["9,000","13,500","10,000","30,000"],"a":2,"x":"Old profit: 90,000 × 40p = £36,000. New profit per drink: 40p − 10p + 6p = 36p. Needed sales: £36,000 ÷ £0.36 = 100,000, so 10,000 extra. The trap is using the old 40p profit.","w":["This divides the lost profit by the old 40p, ignoring that each drink now earns only 36p.","This multiplies the 6p price rise by sales and divides by 40p, ignoring the 10p cost rise.","","This uses 30p profit per drink, forgetting the 6p price rise, so it gives too many drinks."]}
{"id":"gbu-164","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"At which stage of the product life cycle do sales usually rise fastest as more customers hear about the product?","o":["Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Decline"],"a":1,"x":"Sales climb quickly in the growth stage as awareness spreads. In introduction, sales are low. In maturity they level off. In decline they fall.","w":["In introduction few people know the product yet, so sales are low and rise slowly.","","In maturity sales level off near their peak, so this confuses the highest sales with the fastest rise.","In decline sales are falling, not rising, so this mixes up the stage names."]}
{"id":"gbu-165","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is a unique selling point (USP) of a product?","o":["Having the lowest price of any product in the whole market","The number of units of the product sold in one year","A strategy used to lengthen the product's life cycle","A feature that sets it apart from rivals and gives a reason to buy"],"a":3,"x":"A USP is what makes a product different from competitors. It gives customers a reason to choose it. It is not about price alone or sales figures.","w":["A USP need not be the lowest price, as features like quality or design often support higher prices.","Units sold is a sales figure that measures success but does not make the product different.","This describes an extension strategy, not a USP, which is about being different from rivals.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-166","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A bike maker changes its frames to a brighter colour and a smoother, sleeker shape. Which part of the design mix is mainly being changed?","o":["Aesthetics","Function","Cost","Promotion"],"a":0,"x":"Aesthetics is how a product looks, feels, sounds or smells. Colour and shape are aesthetic. Function is about how well it works. Promotion is not part of the design mix.","w":["","Function is how well the bike does its job, and a new colour and shape do not change that.","Cost is about making the product cheaply, and nothing here suggests making the frames cheaper.","Promotion belongs to the marketing mix, not the design mix, so it confuses the two mixes."]}
{"id":"gbu-169","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A new streaming service charges £1 a month for the first three months, then £9. It wants to win customers quickly. Which pricing strategy is this?","o":["Price skimming","Penetration pricing","Psychological pricing","Cost-plus pricing"],"a":1,"x":"Penetration pricing sets a low price when a product launches to win customers and market share fast. The price can rise later. Skimming is the opposite: a high price at launch.","w":["Skimming starts with a high price, but this service starts low, so the two strategies are mixed up.","","Psychological pricing uses prices like £4.99 to look cheaper, not a low launch offer to win customers.","Cost-plus adds a mark-up to unit cost and does not describe a low launch price to gain customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-170","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which product is most suited to price skimming when it first launches?","o":["A new brand of bottled water in a crowded market","A plain loaf of bread sold in supermarkets","A school exercise book","A new patented games console with no close rivals"],"a":3,"x":"Skimming works when a product is new and unique, with few rivals. Some buyers will pay a high price. Everyday products in crowded markets cannot do this, as customers switch to cheaper rivals.","w":["Many rivals sell water, so customers would switch to a cheaper brand; skimming needs few competitors.","Bread is an everyday product with many substitutes; a high price would lose sales.","Exercise books are basic and easy to replace, so customers will not pay a premium.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-171","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A firm makes 1,000 units. Fixed costs are £6,000 and variable costs are £4 per unit. It uses cost-plus pricing with a 25% mark-up. What is the price per unit?","o":["£12.50","£5.00","£7.50","£13.33"],"a":0,"x":"Fixed cost per unit = £6,000 ÷ 1,000 = £6. Total unit cost = £6 + £4 = £10. Mark-up = 25% of £10 = £2.50. Price = £12.50. The trap is using only part of the cost.","w":["","Used only the variable cost (£4 plus 25% = £5), leaving out fixed cost per unit.","Used only the fixed cost per unit (£6 plus 25% = £7.50), leaving out variable cost.","Divided £10 by 0.75, treating 25% as a share of the selling price rather than a mark-up on cost."]}
{"id":"gbu-172","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A bag costs a business £12 to make. It sells for £15. What mark-up percentage does the business use on cost?","o":["3%","20%","25%","80%"],"a":2,"x":"Mark-up is profit as a percentage of cost. Profit = £15 − £12 = £3. £3 ÷ £12 × 100 = 25%. The trap is dividing by the selling price, which gives 20%.","w":["Used the £3 profit as the percentage instead of dividing it by the cost and multiplying by 100.","Divided profit by the selling price (£3 ÷ £15), but mark-up is based on cost, not price.","","Divided cost by selling price (£12 ÷ £15), which is not a mark-up calculation."]}
{"id":"gbu-173","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm launched a phone using price skimming. A year later, three rivals sell similar phones at lower prices. What is the firm most likely to do with its price?","o":["Raise it, because the brand is now well known","Lower it, because competition has increased","Keep it high, because skimming means never changing price","Set it by adding a mark-up to the rivals' prices"],"a":1,"x":"Skimming takes high prices while the product is new and unique. When rivals enter, customers can switch. The firm usually cuts its price to stay competitive. The trap is thinking skimming prices must stay high.","w":["Raising the price when cheaper rivals exist would lose customers; brand awareness does not remove competition.","","Skimming is a launch strategy, and prices are usually lowered later as rivals arrive.","Cost-plus adds a mark-up to the firm's own costs, not to rivals' prices, so this mixes up the methods."]}
{"id":"gbu-174","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A cosmetics firm gives shoppers a free small pot of its new face cream to try. Which method of promotion is this?","o":["A loyalty scheme","Sponsorship","A product trial","A buy one get one free deal"],"a":2,"x":"Giving customers free or low-cost samples to try a product is a product trial. The trap is confusing it with a special offer, which cuts the price of a product the customer still buys.","w":["A loyalty scheme rewards repeat purchases. It does not give free samples to new customers.","Sponsorship means paying to be linked with an event or team, not handing out samples.","","A BOGOF deal is a special offer on items the customer pays for. The free pot here is a sample."]}
{"id":"gbu-175","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A café gives each customer a stamp card. After ten coffees, the next coffee is free. What is the main aim of this loyalty scheme?","o":["To encourage customers to keep coming back","To get people to try the café for the first time","To make customers pay a higher price per coffee","To reduce the cost of buying coffee beans"],"a":0,"x":"Loyalty schemes reward repeat custom, so customers keep returning. The trap is thinking they attract brand-new customers. That is the job of trials or introductory offers.","w":["","Stamp cards only reward people who already buy. They do not attract first-time customers.","Loyalty schemes reward customers. They do not raise prices, and a higher price would put customers off.","Loyalty schemes affect customers, not suppliers. They do not change the cost of ingredients."]}
{"id":"gbu-176","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A sports drink firm pays to have its logo on a local football team's shirts. Which is a likely benefit to the firm?","o":["It guarantees a rise in profit","It gives the firm control over the team's results","It costs the firm nothing","Its brand is seen by many fans, raising awareness"],"a":3,"x":"Sponsorship puts the brand in front of fans at matches and in photos, raising awareness. The trap is thinking it guarantees sales. It is a cost, and results are not certain.","w":["Sponsorship cannot guarantee profit. Fans may still not buy, and the sponsorship fee is a cost.","Sponsors pay for exposure. They do not control what the team does.","Sponsorship is paid for, often at a high price. It is not free.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-177","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A new online clothes shop has £500 to promote itself to 16- to 25-year-olds. Which is the most suitable method and why?","o":["A national TV advert, because TV reaches everyone","Targeted social media adverts, as they reach that age group cheaply","A national newspaper advert, as it is a traditional method","Leaflets sent to every home in the UK, as they are printed"],"a":1,"x":"Social media adverts can be aimed at a chosen age group and fit a small budget. The trap is choosing wide-reach methods like TV, which cost far more and reach many people outside the target market.","w":["National TV adverts cost far more than £500. Most viewers would also be outside the target market.","","National newspaper adverts are expensive. Many readers are not aged 16 to 25, so money is wasted.","Posting leaflets to every UK home costs far more than £500 and is not targeted."]}
{"id":"gbu-178","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A shop sells a jacket for £10 with a unit cost of £6 and sells 500 a week. A 20% off offer doubles sales to 1,000. What is the weekly profit during the offer?","o":["£4,000","£1,600","£2,000","£3,000"],"a":2,"x":"The offer price is £8, so profit per jacket is £8 − £6 = £2. Then 1,000 × £2 = £2,000, the same as before (500 × £4). The trap is thinking that doubling sales doubles profit.","w":["This doubles the old profit and ignores that the price cut lowers the profit on each jacket.","This takes 20% off the old profit. The discount cuts the price, and the lower profit per jacket applies to every sale.","","This is the rise in revenue (£8,000 − £5,000), not profit. It ignores the extra costs."]}
{"id":"gbu-179","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is a benefit to a business of selling through an e-tailer instead of only through high-street shops?","o":["Customers can try the product before they buy","Customers can take the goods home straight away","It can reach customers nationwide without a shop in every town","It never has to pay any delivery costs"],"a":2,"x":"Online selling reaches customers far away without paying rent for many shops. The trap is thinking online selling removes delivery costs. It does not.","w":["Trying products is a benefit of shops. Online customers cannot touch or try items first.","Instant take-home is a shop advantage. Online orders must be delivered, which takes time.","","E-tailers usually pay for delivery or packaging. Thinking online selling is free of delivery costs is a common slip."]}
{"id":"gbu-180","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is a disadvantage for a customer who buys from an e-tailer rather than from a shop?","o":["They cannot see or try the product before buying","They cannot compare prices with other sellers","They can only buy during shop opening hours","They have to travel to a store to pay"],"a":0,"x":"Online customers cannot touch, try on or check a product first. The trap is mixing up online and shop features. Online buying is easy to compare and open all day.","w":["","Online shopping makes price comparison easier, not harder. This mixes up the advantages.","E-tailers are usually open 24 hours. Opening hours limit shops, not websites.","Online customers pay on a website without travelling. This describes a shop visit."]}
{"id":"gbu-181","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Kai makes handmade candles and sells them at a local craft stall. He wants to sell to customers across the UK without high costs. Which place decision fits best?","o":["Rent shops in five big cities","Sell only to people who live nearby","Keep the stall and add more stall days","Set up a website and send orders by post"],"a":3,"x":"A website reaches customers across the UK and costs far less than renting shops. The trap is choosing more physical outlets. These raise costs and still reach only local areas.","w":["Five city shops would give wide reach but at very high rent and wage costs. This does not meet the low-cost aim.","This keeps his market small. Kai wants to reach customers across the UK.","More stall days still only reach people who visit that stall. It does not reach the whole UK.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-183","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A shop costs £3,000 a month to run and has no delivery costs. A website costs £500 a month plus £4 delivery for each order. For 500 orders a month, which is correct?","o":["The shop is cheaper by £500","The website is cheaper by £2,500","The website is cheaper by £500","The website is cheaper by £1,000"],"a":2,"x":"Website cost = £500 + (500 × £4) = £2,500. The shop costs £3,000, so the website is £500 cheaper. The traps are leaving out the fixed cost or the delivery cost.","w":["This reverses the comparison. £2,500 is less than £3,000, so the website is cheaper.","This leaves out the £2,000 delivery cost and only uses £3,000 − £500.","","This leaves out the £500 fixed website cost and only uses £3,000 − £2,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-184","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Why should a business make sure its four Ps work well together?","o":["Only the price needs to be right, because the other three Ps then follow","Each P should be decided on its own, with no link to the others","It guarantees the business will make a profit every year","The Ps support each other and give target customers a clear, consistent message"],"a":3,"x":"A balanced mix means product, price, promotion and place all support each other and fit the target market. It improves the chance of success but never guarantees profit. Setting each P separately risks mixed messages.","w":["Price matters, but thinking one P is enough ignores that the other Ps do not follow automatically.","Separate decisions can clash, such as a luxury product with a low price, because each P affects the others.","Good planning helps, but profit is never guaranteed because costs, competitors and demand can still change.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-185","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A firm sells own-brand sugar. Customers see little difference between brands. Which P is likely to be most important in its mix?","o":["A low, competitive price","Promotion using a celebrity","Product design with a new shape","Place in exclusive shops"],"a":0,"x":"When products are basic and similar, customers choose mainly on price. The trap is assuming promotion or design always matters most. The importance of each P changes with the type of product and market.","w":["","A celebrity adds cost, and customers buying basic sugar rarely choose it because of a famous face.","Changing the design adds little value for a basic product, so it is unlikely to win sales.","Exclusive shops would cut sales of an everyday product, which needs wide, convenient place."]}
{"id":"gbu-186","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A bakery changes from standard bread to a high-quality organic loaf made with costly ingredients. Which change to the rest of the mix makes most sense?","o":["Lower the price to match the cheapest bread on the market","Stop all promotion, because good quality will sell itself","Sell the loaf only through discount market stalls to reach more people","Raise the price and promote the organic quality to health-conscious customers"],"a":3,"x":"Changing the product affects the other Ps. Higher costs and a better product support a higher price. Promotion should tell target customers about the organic quality. Ignoring this link is the trap.","w":["A low price would not cover the higher costs and would clash with the premium product.","Without promotion, many customers will never find out about the new organic quality.","Discount stalls clash with a premium organic image, so place would not fit the target market.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-187","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":2,"q":"An online brand selling trainers targets 16 to 24 year olds with small budgets. Which mix fits this target market best?","o":["Low price, trendy design, social media adverts, sold only in a few luxury city stores","Low price, trendy design, social media adverts, sold on its own website","High price, trendy design, social media adverts, sold on its own website","Low price, old-fashioned design, newspaper adverts, sold on its own website"],"a":1,"x":"Young people with small budgets want low prices, fashionable products, and to be reached on social media and online. Each other option has one P that clashes with the target market.","w":["Luxury stores clash with a low price and are hard for this group to reach.","","A high price does not suit customers with small budgets.","Old-fashioned design and newspapers do not appeal to or reach young people."]}
{"id":"gbu-188","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A bag sells for £50 and costs £20 to make. It sells 1,000 a month. The price is cut to £40 to match a rival, with the same unit cost. What sales are needed to make the same total profit?","o":["1,200 bags","1,250 bags","1,500 bags","2,000 bags"],"a":2,"x":"Current profit is 1,000 × £30 = £30,000. New profit per bag is £20. £30,000 ÷ £20 = 1,500 bags. The trap is thinking a price cut needs only the same percentage rise in sales.","w":["This takes the 20% price cut and adds 20% to sales, ignoring that profit per bag falls by a third.","This uses the price ratio 50 ÷ 40, ignoring that unit cost stays at £20, so profit per bag falls more.","","This assumes profit per bag halves to £15, but it only falls from £30 to £20."]}
{"id":"gbu-189","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A bakery bakes 300 cheese rolls, cleans its equipment, then bakes 300 sausage rolls. Which type of production is this?","o":["Mass production","Batch production","Job production","Flow production"],"a":1,"x":"Batch production makes a set number of identical items, then the equipment is reset for the next batch. The trap is choosing flow or mass production, which run non-stop without switching products.","w":["Mass production makes huge numbers of identical items non-stop; this firm stops to change product.","","Job production makes a single unique item, not 300 identical rolls.","Flow production never stops to switch products; this bakery stops and cleans between products."]}
{"id":"gbu-190","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about providing a service, such as a hairdresser's, is correct?","o":["It can be made in advance and kept in a warehouse","It is a tangible product that the customer takes home","It must always be made using flow production","It cannot be stored, so unused time is lost"],"a":3,"x":"Services are intangible and made as they are used. A hairdresser's empty appointment cannot be saved for later. The trap is treating services like goods that can be stocked.","w":["Services are made when used, so they cannot be kept in stock; this treats a service like a good.","Services are intangible, so there is nothing physical to take home; this confuses a service with a good.","Services are not made on a production line; flow production suits identical goods, not personal services.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-192","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A car factory replaces some assembly workers with robots. Which is a likely disadvantage for the factory in the short run?","o":["High cost of buying and installing the robots","Production becomes slower","The wage bill goes up","More mistakes because robots get tired"],"a":0,"x":"Robots need a large upfront spend on buying, installing and programming. Later, they can raise output and cut wage costs. The trap is thinking robots are slower or less accurate.","w":["","Robots normally work faster and for longer than people, so output usually rises.","Fewer workers usually means a lower wage bill, not a higher one.","Robots do not get tired, so they usually make fewer errors."]}
{"id":"gbu-193","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A firm uses batch production. It installs computer-controlled machines that can be reprogrammed quickly. Which is the most likely benefit?","o":["It must now make only one product","Less time lost when switching between batches","Fixed costs fall because the machines are cheap","It no longer needs any workers"],"a":1,"x":"Computer-controlled machines can be reset by changing the program, so switching products takes less time. This makes the firm more flexible to meet changing demand. The trap is thinking technology means fixed costs fall or all staff go.","w":["Quick reprogramming allows more variety, not just one product; this confuses it with flow production.","","Computer-controlled machines are costly to buy, so fixed costs normally rise, not fall.","Workers are still needed to supervise, maintain and program the machines."]}
{"id":"gbu-194","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":1,"q":"What does the term 'procurement' mean for a business?","o":["Selling finished products to customers","Buying the goods and services it needs from suppliers","Recruiting and training new staff","Making products in its own factory"],"a":1,"x":"Procurement is the buying of the materials, stock and services a business needs, usually from suppliers. Do not mix it up with selling (marketing) or making goods (production).","w":["This is selling or marketing, which deals with customers, not suppliers.","","This is human resources, not buying from suppliers.","This is production, not buying in what the business needs."]}
{"id":"gbu-195","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":1,"q":"A supplier gives a business 30 days of trade credit. What does this mean?","o":["The business gets 30% off its first order","The business must pay 30 days before delivery","The supplier lends the business money for new machinery","The business gets the goods now and pays 30 days later"],"a":3,"x":"Trade credit lets a business receive goods now and pay later. This helps cash flow. It is not a discount, an advance payment or a bank-style loan.","w":["Trade credit is about when you pay, not a percentage discount.","Paying before delivery is the opposite of credit.","Trade credit delays payment for goods; it is not a loan for machinery.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-196","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A shop uses just-in-case stock control and keeps a large amount of stock. What is an advantage of this?","o":["It can still sell if a supplier delivers late","It keeps storage costs low","It cuts the risk of stock going out of date","It frees up cash for other uses"],"a":0,"x":"Just-in-case means holding extra stock, so the business can keep selling if deliveries are late or demand jumps. The trade-offs are higher storage costs, more waste and cash tied up.","w":["","Large stock needs more space, so storage costs rise, not fall.","Holding lots of stock raises the chance of it going out of date or being wasted.","Buying lots of stock ties up cash, so less is free for other uses."]}
{"id":"gbu-197","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A supplier sells an item at £12 each. It gives a 5% discount on the whole order if you buy over 500. A firm orders 600 items. How much does it pay?","o":["£7,195","£360","£6,840","£7,560"],"a":2,"x":"Full price is 600 × £12 = £7,200. The 5% discount is £360. So the firm pays £7,200 − £360 = £6,840. The trap is stopping at the discount amount or adding it on.","w":["This takes off only £5, treating 5% as £5.","£360 is the discount, not the amount paid.","","This adds the discount on instead of taking it off."]}
{"id":"gbu-198","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A bar gate stock graph shows stock falling steadily from 500 to 100 units over 4 weeks. A delivery should arrive when stock reaches 100 units, but it arrives 2 weeks late. How long is the firm out of stock?","o":["No time, because the 100 units cover the delay","1 week","2 weeks","4 weeks"],"a":1,"x":"Stock falls 100 units a week, so the last 100 units last 1 week. With the delivery 2 weeks late, the firm has no stock for 1 week. The trap is counting the whole delay.","w":["The 100 units only last 1 week, so they do not cover a 2-week delay.","","This counts the whole delay and ignores the 1 week the last 100 units last.","Four weeks is the time to use up the stock, not the time spent without stock."]}
{"id":"gbu-199","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is the main reason a business wants to keep the quality of its products high?","o":["It means the business can stop checking products","It lets the business cut the number of workers it needs","It means the business never has to advertise","It builds a good reputation and encourages repeat custom"],"a":3,"x":"High quality means fewer complaints and returns. Customers trust the brand and buy again. This is repeat custom. Quality still needs checking, and it does not remove the need for staff or advertising.","w":["Quality still needs to be checked all the time. High quality does not remove checks.","Quality often needs well-trained staff, so it does not simply reduce worker numbers.","Even high-quality brands advertise. Good quality helps sales but does not replace advertising.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-200","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which action is most likely to improve the quality of service in a café?","o":["Cutting staff numbers to save wages","Training staff in customer service","Using cheaper ingredients to lower costs","Shortening the opening hours"],"a":1,"x":"Training helps staff serve customers politely, quickly and correctly. This improves service quality. The other actions cut costs, but they often lower quality and may drive customers away.","w":["Fewer staff usually means slower service, so quality falls. This confuses cost cutting with quality.","","Cheaper ingredients lower the quality of the food. This is cost cutting, not quality improvement.","Shorter hours reduce when customers can be served. They do not improve the quality of the service."]}
{"id":"gbu-201","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A factory makes 2,000 units a week. 4% are faulty and must be scrapped. Each unit costs £15 to make. What is the weekly cost of the scrapped units?","o":["£60","£0.60","£1,200","£30,000"],"a":2,"x":"4% of 2,000 = 80 faulty units. 80 × £15 = £1,200. The trap is using the whole 2,000 units, or using 4 instead of 80.","w":["Uses 4 faulty units (4 × £15) instead of finding 4% of 2,000.","Finds 4% of the £15 cost, not 4% of the number of units.","","Uses all 2,000 units made (2,000 × £15), not just the 4% that are faulty."]}
{"id":"gbu-202","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A firm only checks products at the very end of production. What is a likely problem with this method of quality control?","o":["Faulty items are found only after time and materials are used on them","It stops all faults from ever happening","It gives workers more say in how they work","It removes the need to throw away any products"],"a":0,"x":"Inspecting at the end finds faults too late. Materials, labour and time have already been spent on the faulty item. Quality assurance tries to prevent faults earlier. End checks do not stop faults happening.","w":["","Control finds faults after they happen. It does not prevent them, which is what assurance aims to do.","Final inspection has nothing to do with worker involvement. This mixes it up with motivation.","Faulty items found at the end still have to be scrapped or reworked, so waste remains."]}
{"id":"gbu-203","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A shoe firm switches to cheaper glue, saving 30p a pair. Soon, customer complaints and returns rise sharply. What is the most likely long-term effect?","o":["Profit will rise because each pair costs less to make","Its reputation weakens, so repeat custom may fall despite lower costs","Its competitive advantage grows because quality no longer matters","Returns will only affect the firm in the first week"],"a":1,"x":"Poor quality damages the brand and makes customers less likely to buy again. Refunds and replacements also add costs. These can outweigh the 30p saving. The trap is looking only at cost per pair.","w":["Looks only at unit cost. Returns, refunds and lost sales may cancel out the 30p saving.","","Customers value quality. Poor quality weakens competitive advantage rather than building it.","Reputation damage lasts. Customers remember poor products and tell others, so effects are not only short term."]}
{"id":"gbu-204","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A shop assistant has good product knowledge. How does this help the sales process?","o":["It means the shop can charge a lower price","It lets staff answer questions and suggest a suitable product","It removes the need for any after-sales support","It means customers never ask for refunds"],"a":1,"x":"Staff who know the products can answer questions and match a product to the customer's needs. This helps close the sale. Trap: good knowledge does not set prices or stop all returns.","w":["Price is set by costs and pricing strategy, not staff knowledge, so this confuses selling with pricing.","","After-sales support is still needed once goods are bought, because product knowledge mainly helps before and during the sale.","Returns can still happen through faults or change of mind, so 'never' is far too strong."]}
{"id":"gbu-205","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of a business using customer engagement?","o":["Ordering more stock from a supplier","Training staff to use a new till","Cutting the price of old stock","Replying to customers' comments on its social media page"],"a":3,"x":"Customer engagement means building a two-way relationship with customers, such as talking with them and responding to them. Ordering stock, till training and price cuts do not involve interacting with customers.","w":["Buying stock from suppliers is an operations decision and does not involve interacting with customers.","Till training is about speed and efficiency of service, not building a relationship with customers.","Cutting prices to clear stock is a pricing decision and does not involve talking with customers.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-206","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A busy café often has long queues. It adds an app so customers can order and pay before arriving. What is the most direct benefit?","o":["Shorter waits, so fewer customers leave without buying","Lower prices on its ingredients","Better product knowledge for the staff","Fewer customers needing after-sales service"],"a":0,"x":"The app speeds up service. Quicker, more efficient service means shorter queues and fewer lost sales. Trap: the app does not change supplier costs or staff knowledge.","w":["","The app does not change what suppliers charge, because ingredient costs come from buying decisions, not ordering technology.","The app changes how orders are taken; it does not teach staff about the products.","The app speeds up service before the sale and does not remove the need for after-sales service."]}
{"id":"gbu-207","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":2,"q":"An online retailer gets many reviews saying 'delivery was late'. Which response best uses customer feedback?","o":["Delete the negative reviews from the website","Spend more on advertising to cover the complaints","Look into the delivery problem, fix it and tell customers","Raise prices to pay for the complaints"],"a":2,"x":"Good use of feedback means finding the cause, putting it right and showing customers it was heard. This keeps customers loyal. Deleting reviews hides the problem and harms trust.","w":["Hiding reviews ignores the feedback, does not fix late delivery and can damage trust if customers notice.","Advertising may attract new customers but the late deliveries continue, so it ignores the complaint.","","Higher prices do not solve late delivery and may drive customers away, so they ignore the feedback."]}
{"id":"gbu-209","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A shop has revenue of £120,000, cost of sales of £70,000 and expenses of £35,000. What is its net profit?","o":["£50,000","£15,000","£85,000","£35,000"],"a":1,"x":"Gross profit = 120,000 - 70,000 = £50,000. Net profit = gross profit - expenses = 50,000 - 35,000 = £15,000. The trap is stopping at gross profit.","w":["This is the gross profit. Expenses have not been taken off yet, so it is not net profit.","","Took expenses off revenue and ignored cost of sales (120,000 - 35,000).","Took expenses off cost of sales (70,000 - 35,000), which is not a profit figure."]}
{"id":"gbu-210","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm has revenue of £200,000, cost of sales of £120,000 and expenses of £50,000. What is its net profit margin?","o":["40%","25%","37.5%","15%"],"a":3,"x":"Gross profit = £80,000. Net profit = 80,000 - 50,000 = £30,000. Net profit margin = 30,000 / 200,000 x 100 = 15%. Always divide by revenue.","w":["This is the gross profit margin (80,000 ÷ 200,000). Expenses were not taken off first.","This is expenses as a % of revenue (50,000 ÷ 200,000), not net profit.","Divided net profit by gross profit (30,000 ÷ 80,000) instead of by revenue.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-211","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":3,"q":"A machine costs £60,000. Over 5 years it brings in a total net return of £135,000, before its cost is taken off. What is the average rate of return (ARR)?","o":["45%","125%","25%","20%"],"a":2,"x":"Total profit = 135,000 - 60,000 = £75,000. Average yearly profit = 75,000 / 5 = £15,000. ARR = 15,000 / 60,000 x 100 = 25%. The trap is forgetting to take off the machine's cost.","w":["Divided the £135,000 return by 5 years without taking off the £60,000 cost, so it is not profit.","Used total profit (75,000 ÷ 60,000) and forgot to divide by 5 years.","","Divided yearly profit by total profit (15,000 ÷ 75,000) instead of by the amount invested."]}
{"id":"gbu-212","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A shop has revenue of £80,000 and cost of sales of £52,000. What is its gross profit margin?","o":["65%","28%","35%","53.8%"],"a":2,"x":"Gross profit = £80,000 − £52,000 = £28,000. Margin = 28,000 ÷ 80,000 × 100 = 35%. The trap is using cost of sales, or dividing by the wrong figure.","w":["This is cost of sales as a percentage of revenue (52,000 ÷ 80,000), not the gross profit margin.","Treated gross profit of £28,000 as a percentage without dividing it by revenue.","","Divided gross profit by cost of sales (28,000 ÷ 52,000) instead of by revenue."]}
{"id":"gbu-213","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A firm spends £50,000 on a new machine. Profits over 4 years are £6,000, £10,000, £12,000 and £12,000. What is the average rate of return (ARR)?","o":["20%","80%","24%","12%"],"a":0,"x":"Total profit is £40,000. Average yearly profit is £40,000 ÷ 4 = £10,000. ARR = 10,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = 20%. The trap is forgetting to average the profit.","w":["","Used total profit (£40,000) without dividing by 4 years to find the average.","Used only the final year's profit of £12,000 instead of the average yearly profit.","Used only the first year's profit of £6,000 instead of the average yearly profit."]}
{"id":"gbu-214","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A business has revenue of £200,000, cost of sales of £120,000 and expenses of £60,000. What is its net profit margin?","o":["40%","30%","16.7%","10%"],"a":3,"x":"Gross profit = £80,000. Net profit = £80,000 − £60,000 = £20,000. Net margin = 20,000 ÷ 200,000 × 100 = 10%. The trap is stopping at gross profit.","w":["This is the gross profit margin (80,000 ÷ 200,000); expenses were not taken off.","Divided expenses by revenue (60,000 ÷ 200,000), which is not a profit margin.","Divided net profit by cost of sales (20,000 ÷ 120,000) instead of by revenue.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-215","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"Both projects cost £100,000 and last 3 years with total profit £60,000. A earns £40,000, £20,000, £0. B earns £0, £20,000, £40,000. What does this show about ARR?","o":["Project A is better because it has a higher ARR","ARR ignores when profits arrive, so it cannot tell A and B apart","Project B is better because its biggest profit comes last","ARR cannot be used because the projects cost the same"],"a":1,"x":"Both have an ARR of 20,000 ÷ 100,000 = 20%. ARR ignores the timing of profits, though A returns money sooner. That is a limitation of ARR.","w":["The ARR is equal at 20% for both, so A does not have a higher ARR.","","The ARR is the same, and ARR does not reward later profits, so this is not a valid conclusion.","ARR can be used when costs are equal; the real limitation is that it ignores timing."]}
{"id":"gbu-216","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":1,"q":"In an organisational structure, what is meant by the 'chain of command'?","o":["The route that orders pass along, from senior managers down to junior staff","The number of workers that a manager is directly responsible for","The number of levels of management in a business","How decisions are shared between head office and local branches"],"a":0,"x":"The chain of command is the line of authority through which instructions pass from the top to the bottom. Do not confuse it with span of control, which counts the staff a manager supervises.","w":["","That is span of control, a similar term that is often mixed up with the chain of command.","That describes the layers of hierarchy, not the line along which orders are passed.","That describes centralised or decentralised decision making, not the chain of command."]}
{"id":"gbu-217","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":1,"q":"A business has a chain of shops. Which is an advantage of a centralised structure?","o":["Branch managers feel more trusted and so are more motivated","Decisions are made faster inside each shop","Prices and rules stay the same in every shop","Decisions are based on better local knowledge"],"a":2,"x":"In a centralised structure, head office makes the key decisions, so shops follow the same policies. The other benefits listed (speed, trust, local knowledge) belong to decentralised structures.","w":["Trust and motivation come from giving managers power, which is decentralised, not centralised.","Fast local decisions come from decentralisation, as centralised decisions often wait for head office.","","Using local knowledge is a benefit of decentralisation, because head office is further from customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-218","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A manager supervises 3 team leaders. Each team leader supervises 8 shop assistants. What is the span of control of one team leader?","o":["3","8","11","24"],"a":1,"x":"Span of control is the number of people a manager directly supervises. Each team leader directly supervises 8 assistants. The trap is adding or multiplying the levels together.","w":["3 is the span of control of the top manager, not of a team leader.","","11 comes from adding 3 and 8, but span of control counts only direct subordinates.","24 is the total number of shop assistants, not those one team leader supervises."]}
{"id":"gbu-219","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A design firm lets all its staff work from home. Which is a likely disadvantage for the firm?","o":["It must pay higher rent for a bigger office","It can only recruit staff who live near the firm","Staff cannot use email or video calls to share ideas","It is harder to supervise staff and build team spirit"],"a":3,"x":"With remote working, managers cannot see staff at work and staff meet less often, so supervision and team spirit can suffer. Lower office costs and a wider recruitment area are advantages, not disadvantages.","w":["Remote working usually reduces rent because less office space is needed, so this is wrong.","Remote working widens the area a firm can recruit from, so this is the wrong way round.","Staff can still use email and video calls from home; the real problem is less face-to-face contact.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-220","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":3,"q":"A firm with six layers of management removes one layer of middle managers. The number of staff stays the same. What is the most likely result?","o":["Remaining managers get a wider span of control and messages pass through fewer layers","Remaining managers get a narrower span of control and the chain of command grows longer","Messages take longer because they pass through more people before reaching staff","Managers supervise fewer staff, so they will need to delegate less work"],"a":0,"x":"Removing a layer makes the structure flatter. The same staff are shared among fewer managers, so spans widen and the chain of command shortens. Managers then often need to delegate more.","w":["","This mixes up flat and tall structures, as fewer layers with the same staff gives a wider span and shorter chain.","Fewer layers means fewer people to pass messages through, so communication is usually quicker.","With the same staff and fewer managers, each manager supervises more people, so delegation increases."]}
{"id":"gbu-221","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of recruiting a supervisor from inside the business rather than from outside?","o":["It brings in fresh ideas from other businesses","It gives the business a much wider choice of applicants","The person already knows the business, so less training may be needed","It means no other job needs to be filled"],"a":2,"x":"An internal recruit already knows the business, its products and its staff, so induction can be shorter. Fresh ideas and wider choice are advantages of external recruitment. Promotion often leaves a gap to fill.","w":["Fresh ideas are an advantage of external recruitment, because outsiders bring different experience.","A wider choice of applicants comes from external recruitment; internal recruitment only draws on current staff.","","Promoting someone usually creates a new vacancy in their old job, so another job still needs filling."]}
{"id":"gbu-222","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A shop is hiring a sales assistant. Which item would be found in the person specification?","o":["Good communication skills and some customer service experience","Serving customers and handling cash at the till","The applicant's past jobs and school grades","The job title, pay and hours of work"],"a":0,"x":"A person specification lists the skills, qualifications and experience the ideal candidate should have. Tasks belong in the job description. Past jobs and grades come from the applicant's CV.","w":["","Serving customers and handling cash are tasks, so they belong in the job description, not the person specification.","Past jobs and grades are details about one applicant, found on a CV, not the qualities the business wants.","Title, pay and hours are job details usually shown in the job description or advert, not the person specification."]}
{"id":"gbu-223","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A café needs a new manager. The owner wants someone who already knows the menu and the team, and wants to fill the post quickly at low cost. Which is the best method?","o":["Advertise in a national newspaper","Pay a recruitment agency to find someone","Advertise on an online job website","Promote a current supervisor"],"a":3,"x":"Promoting a supervisor is internal recruitment. It is quick and cheap, and the person already knows the menu and team. The other three are external methods that cost money and take time, and the new person would not know the café.","w":["A national newspaper advert is external, costly and slow, and applicants would not know the café.","An agency charges a fee and finds outsiders, so it is not the cheapest or the quickest way.","A job website is external, so applicants would not already know the menu or the team.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-224","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":2,"q":"Why might a business ask applicants to fill in an application form instead of sending only a CV?","o":["It lists the tasks the new employee will carry out","Every applicant gives the same information in the same order","Applicants can choose which details to include","It sets out the qualities the business wants in a candidate"],"a":1,"x":"An application form asks every applicant the same questions, so the business can compare them easily. A CV is set out by each applicant, so details and layout differ. Tasks and qualities wanted are in the job description and person specification.","w":["Listing tasks is the job of the job description, not an application form.","","Choosing which details to include is a feature of a CV, which is why forms are easier to compare.","Qualities wanted are in the person specification, not the application form."]}
{"id":"gbu-225","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm uses a recruitment agency to fill a post paying £32,500 a year. The agency charges 12% of the first-year salary. The firm also pays £450 to advertise on its own website. What is the total recruitment cost?","o":["£3,900","£840","£4,350","£36,850"],"a":2,"x":"12% of £32,500 is £3,900. Add the £450 advert to get £4,350. The trap is forgetting a cost or adding the salary, which is not a recruitment cost.","w":["This is only the agency fee; it leaves out the £450 website advert.","This uses 1.2% instead of 12%, giving £390, then adds £450, so the percentage is wrong.","","This adds the salary to the costs, but the salary is a wage cost, not a recruitment cost."]}
{"id":"gbu-226","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of formal training?","o":["A new cashier watching a colleague serve customers","A worker attending a first-aid course at a local college","A worker learning a new app from online videos at home","A trainee picking up tips from a mentor while in the shop"],"a":1,"x":"Formal training is planned and structured, such as a course with set content. Learning by watching colleagues is informal training, and teaching yourself is self-learning. The trap is thinking any learning is formal training.","w":["Watching a colleague while working is informal training, not a planned, structured course.","","Teaching yourself from online videos is self-learning, not formal training organised by the business.","Picking up tips from a mentor while working is informal, unstructured training."]}
{"id":"gbu-227","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A manager sets a target for a sales assistant. Which target is SMART?","o":["Try hard to sell more items this year","Become the best seller in the whole shop","Sell lots more phone cases very soon","Sell 40 phone cases a week by the end of March"],"a":3,"x":"A SMART target is specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and time-bound. Only the last one gives a number and a deadline. The trap is choosing a target that sounds ambitious but cannot be measured.","w":["'More' is not a number and 'this year' is vague, so it is not specific or measurable.","'Best seller' has no clear measure or deadline and depends on other people, so it is not SMART.","'Lots more' and 'very soon' are vague, so the target is not measurable or time-bound.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-228","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":2,"q":"At a performance review, a café manager finds a barista makes drinks too slowly. What is the most suitable outcome of the review?","o":["Agree a training session on the coffee machine and a target time for drinks","Sack the barista straight away to set an example","Give a pay rise so the barista works faster","Wait until next year's induction before saying anything"],"a":0,"x":"Performance reviews identify strengths and weaknesses, then agree training and new targets. This helps the worker improve. The trap is thinking a review is only for punishing staff or handing out pay.","w":["","Reviews are meant to help staff improve, not to sack them; dismissal skips training and targets.","A pay rise does not teach the skill the barista is missing; this confuses motivation with training need.","Delaying feedback for a year wastes time; induction is for new starters, not for fixing current performance."]}
{"id":"gbu-230","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":3,"q":"A firm sends 20 workers on a 2-day course. The course costs £150 per worker. Each worker earns £90 a day and does no work for the firm on the course. The trainer charges £500 extra. Total cost?","o":["£6,600","£7,100","£3,500","£4,100"],"a":1,"x":"Course fees: 20 × £150 = £3,000. Wages for lost work: 20 × 2 × £90 = £3,600. Trainer: £500. Total £7,100. The trap is leaving out one of the three costs, especially wages paid for time not worked.","w":["This leaves out the trainer's £500 fee, so the total is too low.","","This leaves out the wages paid for the 2 days (£3,600), which are a real cost of training.","This leaves out the £3,000 course fees, counting only wages and the trainer."]}
{"id":"gbu-231","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is a likely benefit to a business of having a well-motivated workforce?","o":["Higher labour turnover","Lower labour turnover","More days lost through absence","Higher recruitment and training costs"],"a":1,"x":"Motivated staff are more likely to stay, so labour turnover falls. The trap is thinking motivation raises turnover, absence or recruitment costs. Those are signs of low motivation.","w":["Higher labour turnover means more staff leaving, which is a sign of low motivation, not high.","","More absence is linked to poorly motivated staff, so it is not a benefit.","Recruitment and training costs rise when staff leave, so this is a cost of low motivation."]}
{"id":"gbu-232","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A sales worker earns a salary of £1,200 a month plus 4% commission on sales. This month she sells £15,000 of goods. What is her total pay?","o":["£1,800","£600","£1,260","£16,200"],"a":0,"x":"Commission = 4% of £15,000 = £600. Add the £1,200 salary to get £1,800. The traps are giving only the commission, slipping a decimal place, or adding the salary to sales instead of commission.","w":["","This is only the commission. It leaves out the £1,200 salary.","This uses 4% of £1,500, a decimal slip, so the commission is far too small.","This adds the salary to the sales figure instead of to the commission."]}
{"id":"gbu-233","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A shop lets its team decide their own rota and solve customer problems without asking a manager first. Which motivation method is this?","o":["Job rotation","Fringe benefits","Piece rate","Autonomy"],"a":3,"x":"Autonomy gives workers freedom to make their own decisions. It is a non-financial method. The trap is job rotation: it moves staff between tasks but does not give them more control.","w":["Job rotation moves staff between different tasks. Nothing here says they swap jobs.","Fringe benefits are perks such as a staff discount. They are not about decision-making.","Piece rate is pay per item made, so it is financial and not about control.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-fig-005","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"The bars show a firm's closing cash balance each month. In April, cash outflows were £35,000. Use the graph to find April's cash inflows.","o":["£85,000","£15,000","£65,000","£55,000"],"a":2,"x":"March's closing balance is April's opening balance: £20,000. April closing is £50,000. Inflows = 50,000 - 20,000 + 35,000 = £65,000.","w":["Adds April's closing balance to the outflows and ignores the opening balance.","Takes outflows away from the closing balance and ignores the opening balance.","","Uses February's closing balance (£30,000) as the opening balance instead of March's."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May"],"vals":[20,30,20,50,40],"y":[0,60],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"month","ylabel":"closing balance / £000"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-006","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a firm's forecast closing cash balance. Its bank allows an overdraft of £20,000. At the worst point, by how much does the forecast go beyond this limit?","o":["£10,000","£30,000","£20,000","£50,000"],"a":0,"x":"The lowest point is month 4, at -£30,000. The limit is -£20,000. The firm is short by 30,000 - 20,000 = £10,000 beyond its overdraft.","w":["","Gives the size of the lowest balance and forgets to take off the overdraft limit.","Gives the overdraft limit itself, not the amount beyond it.","Adds the limit to the lowest balance instead of finding the difference."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1,6],"y":[-40,30],"xstep":1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"month number (1 = January)","ylabel":"closing balance / £000","lines":[{"pts":[[1,20],[2,10],[3,-10],[4,-30],[5,-10],[6,10]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-007","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Line A is a firm's forecast closing balance if customers pay on credit. Line B is the forecast if they pay at once. In which months does only A show a negative balance?","o":["Months 2, 3 and 4","Month 3 only","Months 1 to 3","Months 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"A is below zero in month 2 (-£10,000) and month 3 (-£20,000). In month 4 it is exactly zero, so not negative. B stays above zero in every month.","w":["Counts month 4 as negative, but A is at zero there, not below it.","Picks only the lowest point and misses month 2, where A is also below zero.","Includes month 1, where A is still positive at £10,000.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1,6],"y":[-30,40],"xstep":1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"month number (1 = January)","ylabel":"closing balance / £000","lines":[{"pts":[[1,10],[2,-10],[3,-20],[4,0],[5,20],[6,30]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[1,20],[2,10],[3,10],[4,20],[5,30],[6,40]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-001","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Use the graph. At what output does the business break even?","o":["20,000 units","2,000 units","4,000 units","8,000 units"],"a":1,"x":"Break-even is where the revenue line crosses the total costs line. Reading down from that crossing to the output axis gives 2,000 units. The money value there is £20,000.","w":["Reads the money value at the crossing (£20,000) off the vertical axis and calls it units.","","Gives the right-hand end of the output axis, not the point where the two lines cross.","Reads the fixed costs (£8,000), where the total costs line starts, instead of the crossing point."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4000],"y":[0,40000],"xstep":500,"ystep":5000,"xlabel":"output / units","ylabel":"revenue and costs / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[4000,40000]],"label":"Revenue","c":0},{"pts":[[0,8000],[4000,32000]],"label":"Total costs","c":1},{"pts":[[0,8000],[4000,8000]],"label":"Fixed costs","dash":true,"c":2}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-002","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Point P on the graph shows this month's sales. Use the graph to work out the margin of safety.","o":["800 units","600 units","200 units","1,400 units"],"a":2,"x":"Break-even is where the lines cross, at 600 units. Point P is at 800 units. Margin of safety = actual sales − break-even output = 800 − 600 = 200 units.","w":["Gives the actual sales at P. The margin of safety is the gap between sales and break-even, not the sales figure.","Gives the break-even output only. The break-even output needs to be taken away from the sales at P.","","Adds sales and break-even together instead of subtracting break-even from sales."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,1000],"y":[0,15000],"xstep":200,"ystep":3000,"xlabel":"output / units","ylabel":"revenue and costs / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[1000,15000]],"label":"Revenue","c":0},{"pts":[[0,6000],[1000,11000]],"label":"Total costs","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[800,12000],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-003","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Use the graph. What is the business's profit or loss when it makes 1,000 units?","o":["Loss of £5,000","Profit of £5,000","Loss of £10,000","Loss of £15,000"],"a":0,"x":"At 1,000 units, revenue is £10,000 and total costs are £15,000. Costs are higher than revenue, so it is a loss. Loss = 15,000 − 10,000 = £5,000.","w":["","Finds the gap of £5,000 but gets the sign wrong. Total costs are above revenue here, so this is a loss.","Reads only the revenue (or the fixed costs) as the loss, and does not find the gap between the lines.","Reads only the total costs as the loss, and does not take revenue away."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,3000],"y":[0,30000],"xstep":500,"ystep":5000,"xlabel":"output / units","ylabel":"revenue and costs / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[3000,30000]],"label":"Revenue","c":0},{"pts":[[0,10000],[3000,25000]],"label":"Total costs","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-004","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Fixed costs are £20,000 each month. Variable costs are 40% of revenue. Use the chart to find the profit in the month with the highest revenue.","o":["£40,000","£36,000","£24,000","£16,000"],"a":3,"x":"The highest bar is March, with revenue of £60,000. Variable costs = 40% of £60,000 = £24,000. Profit = 60,000 − 24,000 − 20,000 = £16,000.","w":["Takes away the fixed costs only (60,000 − 20,000) and forgets the variable costs.","Takes away the variable costs only (60,000 − 24,000) and forgets the fixed costs.","Gives the variable costs (40% of revenue) instead of the profit.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr"],"vals":[40,50,60,30],"y":[0,70],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"month","ylabel":"revenue / £000"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-011","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a shop's revenue for four years. Revenue fell in one year. What was the percentage fall in revenue in that year?","o":["50%","25%","20%","80%"],"a":2,"x":"The graph shows revenue falling from £250,000 in Year 3 to £200,000 in Year 4. The fall is £50,000. Percentage fall = 50 ÷ 250 × 100 = 20%.","w":["Used the fall in £000 (50) as a percentage. You must divide by the earlier figure and multiply by 100.","Divided the fall by the new revenue (200) instead of the earlier revenue (250).","","Found Year 4 revenue as a percentage of Year 3 (200 ÷ 250) instead of the fall."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4"],"vals":[150,200,250,200],"y":[0,300],"ystep":50,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"revenue / £000"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-012","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the net profit margin of two businesses, A and B, over five years. Which statement about the two businesses is correct?","o":["A's net profit margin was higher than B's in Years 4 and 5","B made more profit in pounds in Year 1 because its margin was higher","The two margins were equal in both Year 2 and Year 3","B's net profit margin rose in every year"],"a":0,"x":"In Years 4 and 5 the line for A is above the line for B (20% against 15%, then 25% against 10%). The lines meet only in Year 3. B's line never rises. Margins do not show profit in pounds.","w":["","A higher margin does not mean more profit in pounds. The graph gives no revenue figures, so you cannot compare pounds.","The lines meet only in Year 3. In Year 2 the margins are different.","B's margin fell or stayed the same each year. It never rose. You misread the direction of the line."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1,5],"y":[0,30],"xstep":1,"ystep":5,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"net profit margin / %","lines":[{"pts":[[1,10],[2,15],[3,20],[4,20],[5,25]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[1,25],[2,20],[3,20],[4,15],[5,10]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-013","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A business spent £200,000 on a new machine. The graph shows the net return the machine earned each year. What is the average rate of return (ARR) over the four years?","o":["100%","12.5%","50%","25%"],"a":3,"x":"From the graph, returns are £60,000, £100,000, £100,000 and £140,000. Total = £400,000. Total profit = £400,000 − £200,000 = £200,000. Average per year = £50,000. ARR = 50 ÷ 200 × 100 = 25%.","w":["Divided total profit (£200,000) by the cost but forgot to divide by 4 years first.","Divided the average yearly profit by the total returns (400) instead of the £200,000 cost.","Used the average yearly return (£100,000) and did not take off the £200,000 cost.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4"],"vals":[60,100,100,140],"y":[0,160],"ystep":20,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"net return / £000"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-016","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a firm's yearly net profit from a new machine that cost £160,000. What is the average rate of return (ARR) over the 5 years?","o":["62.5%","18.75%","12.5%","20%"],"a":2,"x":"Read the five bars: 10 + 15 + 20 + 25 + 30 = £100,000 total profit. Average per year = £20,000. ARR = 20,000 ÷ 160,000 × 100 = 12.5%.","w":["Divided the total profit by the cost but forgot to divide by 5 years first.","Used only the Year 5 bar (£30,000) instead of the average of all five bars.","","Took the average bar height of 20 and wrote it as a percentage without dividing by the cost."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4","Year 5"],"vals":[10,15,20,25,30],"y":[0,40],"ystep":5,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"net profit / £000"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-017","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a business's revenue and total costs. Use points P and Q, at the same output, to work out the net profit margin at that output.","o":["33.3%","25%","75%","14.3%"],"a":1,"x":"At P, revenue is £8,000. At Q, total costs are £6,000. Net profit = 8,000 − 6,000 = £2,000. Net profit margin = 2,000 ÷ 8,000 × 100 = 25%.","w":["Divided the profit by total costs instead of by revenue.","","Found total costs as a percentage of revenue (6,000 ÷ 8,000), not the profit.","Divided the profit by revenue and costs added together instead of by revenue only."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,500],"y":[0,10000],"xstep":100,"ystep":2000,"xlabel":"output / units","ylabel":"money / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[500,10000]],"label":"Revenue","c":0},{"pts":[[0,2000],[500,7000]],"label":"Total costs","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[400,8000],"label":"P"},{"at":[400,6000],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-014","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the results of a survey for a new lunch shop. Each person asked chose one item. What percentage of all the people asked chose wraps?","o":["24%","40%","30%","75%"],"a":2,"x":"Add the bars to find everyone asked: 32 + 16 + 8 + 24 = 80. Wraps is 24 people. 24 ÷ 80 = 0.3, so 30%.","w":["Used the bar height of 24 as a percentage without dividing by the total number of people asked.","Used the sandwich bar, 32 out of 80, instead of the wrap bar.","","Divided the wrap bar by the tallest bar instead of by the total of all the bars."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Sandwich","Salad","Soup","Wrap"],"vals":[32,16,8,24],"y":[0,40],"ystep":8,"xlabel":"lunch item chosen","ylabel":"number of people"}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-015","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A firm uses this secondary data to study a market. In which year did sales rise by the most compared with the year before?","o":["2022","2020","2024","2021"],"a":0,"x":"Find the rise each year. From 2021 to 2022 sales go from 400 to 700, up £300 million. This is the steepest section. Every other year rises by £100 million.","w":["","Picked the first year of growth. That rise is only £100 million.","Chose the year with the highest sales value, not the biggest rise.","Picked the year at the start of the steepest section, not the year the rise ends."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[2019,2024],"y":[0,900],"xstep":1,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"sales of plant-based drinks / £ million","lines":[{"pts":[[2019,200],[2020,300],[2021,400],[2022,700],[2023,800],[2024,900]],"c":0}]}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-008","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The firm plans an extension strategy at the last moment before sales begin to fall. Which labelled point on the graph is closest to that time?","o":["P, because sales are still very low and need a boost","Q, because sales are rising at their fastest rate","R, because sales have levelled off and are about to fall","S, because sales are already falling quickly"],"a":2,"x":"The graph shows sales peaking and flattening at R, just before they drop. An extension strategy is best used here to lengthen the life cycle. Q is growth, S is decline, P is introduction.","w":["Picks P, the introduction stage. Low sales here are normal for a new product and do not mean sales are about to fall.","Picks Q, where the line is steepest. This is growth, with sales still rising, so there is no fall to prevent yet.","","Picks S, where sales are already well into decline. This is too late to be the last moment before sales begin to fall."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,120],"xstep":1,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / years","ylabel":"sales / thousand units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[1,20],[2,40],[3,80],[4,100],[5,100],[6,80],[7,40],[8,20]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[1,20],"label":"P"},{"at":[3,80],"label":"Q"},{"at":[5,100],"label":"R"},{"at":[7,40],"label":"S"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-009","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The firm relaunched the product in year 5. In year 7, how many more units were sold with the relaunch than without it, according to the graph?","o":["80 000 units","60 000 units","20 000 units","40 000 units"],"a":1,"x":"At year 7 the relaunch line is at 80 thousand and the no-relaunch line is at 20 thousand. The difference is 60 thousand, which is 60 000 units.","w":["Reads only the relaunch line at year 7. The question asks for the extra sales, so the other line must be taken away.","","Reads only the no-relaunch line at year 7. This is the sales without the relaunch, not the extra sales.","Finds the gap at year 6 instead of year 7. The gap changes as the lines move apart."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,120],"xstep":1,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / years","ylabel":"sales / thousand units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[1,20],[2,60],[3,100],[4,100],[5,80],[6,40],[7,20],[8,0]],"label":"No relaunch","c":0},{"pts":[[5,80],[6,80],[7,80],[8,60]],"dash":true,"label":"Relaunch","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbu-fig-010","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A phone case firm says its USP is its design. 200 customers each gave one main reason for buying. How many customers gave the USP as their reason, according to the graph?","o":["80 customers","40 customers","60 customers","120 customers"],"a":0,"x":"The design bar is at 40%. 40% of 200 customers is 0.4 × 200 = 80 customers. The axis shows percentages, not numbers of customers.","w":["","Reads 40 off the axis as a number of customers. The axis is in percent, so it must be turned into a share of 200.","Reads the brand bar (30%) instead of the design bar. The USP is the design feature.","Works out the share who did not choose design (60%) instead of the share who did."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Price","Design","Quality","Brand"],"vals":[20,40,10,30],"y":[0,50],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"main reason for buying","ylabel":"customers / %"}}
